494
00:28:31,978 --> 00:28:34,280
engaging in another of a series
of courtroom gymnastics.

495
00:28:34,313 --> 00:28:36,415
- Your honor,
I'm simply trying to prove

496
00:28:36,448 --> 00:28:39,318
that Mr. Cody is nothing more
than a fictional character

497
00:28:39,352 --> 00:28:40,520
in a lame series.

498
00:28:40,553 --> 00:28:42,388
Or a lame character
in a fictional series.

499
00:28:42,422 --> 00:28:45,057
- Your honor, I shall prove
beyond a shadow of a doubt

500
00:28:45,090 --> 00:28:47,293
that Commando Cody
is in fact real.

501
00:28:47,327 --> 00:28:49,095
I have here millions of letters

502
00:28:49,128 --> 00:28:51,130
delivered by the U.S. Postal
Service who are never,

503
00:28:51,163 --> 00:28:52,532
or at least seldom, wrong.

504
00:28:52,565 --> 00:28:55,368
Letters from children of America
who believe in Cody

505
00:28:55,402 --> 00:28:56,837
and what he stands for.

506
00:28:56,870 --> 00:28:58,838
- I--I object, your honor.

507
00:28:58,872 --> 00:29:01,073
My case on Cody is based on
hearsay and rumor.

508
00:29:01,107 --> 00:29:04,110
- Really? I thought it was
shoddy planning and forethought.

509
00:29:04,143 --> 00:29:05,478
- Well, they helped.
- Mr. Servo,

510
00:29:05,512 --> 00:29:07,847
where are these letters
of which you speak?

511
00:29:07,880 --> 00:29:10,383
- Hit is Cambot.

512
00:29:10,417 --> 00:29:13,285
- Oh! I don't believe this.

513
00:29:13,318 --> 00:29:15,120
- Out of order?
You're out of order!

514
00:29:15,153 --> 00:29:16,823
He's out of order!
We're all out of order!

515
00:29:16,857 --> 00:29:19,292
This courtroom is out of order!
This country is out of order!

516
00:29:19,325 --> 00:29:21,527
- We've got commercial sign.
Let's go.

517
00:29:21,560 --> 00:29:22,895
- Out of order!

518
00:29:30,470 --> 00:29:31,938
- I'm ready to believe you.

519
00:29:31,972 --> 00:29:34,873
But slime men who build
a dome...

520
00:29:34,907 --> 00:29:36,977
- Here we go again.

521
00:29:37,010 --> 00:29:40,880
- Okay. But you've never
seen them, right?

522
00:29:40,913 --> 00:29:42,015
- No.

523
00:29:42,048 --> 00:29:43,482
- Well, maybe we can.

524
00:29:43,515 --> 00:29:46,518
I'm a newscaster. Sports mostly.

525
00:29:46,552 --> 00:29:48,320
- So I work with slime
all the time.

526
00:29:48,353 --> 00:29:50,022
- ...bound to be film on this.

527
00:29:50,055 --> 00:29:51,457
- Oh, good.

528
00:29:51,490 --> 00:29:53,158
- That Tom Gregory.

529
00:29:53,192 --> 00:29:54,527
- How about it, Professor?

530
00:29:54,560 --> 00:29:56,428
- I think it's a splendid idea.

531
00:29:56,462 --> 00:29:58,363
- We can turn it in to
a road trip.

532
00:29:58,397 --> 00:30:00,300
- We may be able to get some
additional data

533
00:30:00,333 --> 00:30:02,268
that would be invaluable to us.

534
00:30:04,337 --> 00:30:07,473
- And could you stop
at a Walmart? I need some Tums.

535
00:30:12,478 --> 00:30:14,280
- I'll be darn.

536
00:30:14,313 --> 00:30:17,583
Sorry, Professor, guess
I didn't want to believe you.

537
00:30:17,617 --> 00:30:20,920
Here we are. Slime people.

538
00:30:20,953 --> 00:30:22,888
- 1964. Robert Hutton, director.

539
00:30:22,922 --> 00:30:24,857
A Golan Globus production.

540
00:30:24,890 --> 00:30:27,160
- Why don't you put it on
and skip to the end,

541
00:30:27,193 --> 00:30:28,628
and we can all get out of here?

542
00:30:28,662 --> 00:30:31,363
- Dad, why does all of this
have to happen to us?

543
00:30:31,397 --> 00:30:34,033
- Why did a goofy old
apocalypse have to happen

544
00:30:34,067 --> 00:30:36,235
right when my acne
was clearing up?

545
00:30:36,268 --> 00:30:40,105
- Now let's watch this film
and see if we can learn anything

546
00:30:40,138 --> 00:30:42,975
about their mysterious wall.

547
00:30:43,008 --> 00:30:44,343
- Oh, good.

548
00:30:44,377 --> 00:30:46,512
Maybe it'll be a Chilly Willy
or a Droopy cartoon.

549
00:30:46,545 --> 00:30:48,247
- Here's a late bulletin,
ladies and gentlemen.

550
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:50,617
- It's Droopie.
- ...have been found murdered

551
00:30:50,650 --> 00:30:52,485
on and near the beaches tonight.

552
00:30:52,518 --> 00:30:55,322
All beach communities
are being alerted.

553
00:30:55,355 --> 00:30:59,558
If you are in the beach area,
do not leave your home.

554
00:30:59,592 --> 00:31:03,228
As a special service,
I'm informed now

555
00:31:03,262 --> 00:31:05,598
that we are setting up
a remote unit on the spot.

556
00:31:05,632 --> 00:31:08,433
And we'll bring you firsthand
news of this tragedy.

557
00:31:08,467 --> 00:31:10,503
Stay tuned for future bulletins.

558
00:31:10,537 --> 00:31:12,238
- But first a beauty makeover.

559
00:31:12,272 --> 00:31:13,973
- We're at the scene
of still another murder...

560
00:31:14,007 --> 00:31:16,575
- How'd he get there?
He was just in the other place.

561
00:31:16,608 --> 00:31:19,112
- ...a young engineer was found
not 20 minutes ago in this spot.

562
00:31:19,145 --> 00:31:20,480
Is Ms. Castillo here?

563
00:31:20,513 --> 00:31:22,215
- An old Orson Welles gag.

564
00:31:22,248 --> 00:31:23,983
- You witnessed this,
didn't you?

565
00:31:24,017 --> 00:31:27,653
- Yes, from my window. I saw it.

566
00:31:27,687 --> 00:31:30,022
- They're talking
to a Pez dispenser.

567
00:31:30,055 --> 00:31:33,292
- That man,
he got out of his car.

568
00:31:33,325 --> 00:31:36,062
And they came at him
from the sewers

569
00:31:36,095 --> 00:31:37,997
- Well here, have Pez.

570
00:31:38,030 --> 00:31:39,532
- The things! They killed him.

571
00:31:39,565 --> 00:31:42,435
And he screamed!

572
00:31:42,468 --> 00:31:43,670
- Make you feel better.

573
00:31:43,703 --> 00:31:46,172
- Ms. Castillo. Ms. Castillo.

574
00:31:46,205 --> 00:31:48,507
That was Ms. Castillo,
eyewitness to the latest murder.

575
00:31:48,540 --> 00:31:50,943
We'll stay by our unit to hear
at our mobile unit

576
00:31:50,977 --> 00:31:53,278
for further reports.
You stay tuned to KTTV.

577
00:31:53,312 --> 00:31:54,680
Vince Williams signing off
for now.

578
00:31:54,713 --> 00:31:57,983
The report of large,
monstrous creatures--

579
00:31:58,017 --> 00:31:59,418
- It's the All Vince network.

580
00:31:59,452 --> 00:32:02,522
- All Vince, all the time.

581
00:32:02,555 --> 00:32:07,027
- The military forces today
evacuated all the beach areas.

582
00:32:07,060 --> 00:32:09,628
They are now engaged in
hand-to-hand combat...

583
00:32:09,662 --> 00:32:11,363
- It's a hand-to-fin combat.

584
00:32:11,397 --> 00:32:15,233
It's believed that they are
all within this area now,

585
00:32:15,267 --> 00:32:16,568
north of Santa Monica.

586
00:32:16,602 --> 00:32:17,368
- Right there.

587
00:32:17,402 --> 00:32:18,503
- However,
residents of all areas

588
00:32:18,537 --> 00:32:22,108
are warned to stay indoors
and barricade their homes.

589
00:32:22,142 --> 00:32:24,343
Do not attempt,
according to authorities,

590
00:32:24,377 --> 00:32:27,513
to evacuate at this time.
Stay in your homes.

591
00:32:27,547 --> 00:32:29,315
- If it's yellow, let it mellow.

592
00:32:29,348 --> 00:32:30,717
- Should be over
by this morning.

593
00:32:30,750 --> 00:32:32,585
Stay indoors tonight.

594
00:32:32,618 --> 00:32:35,688
We've canceled all broadcasts
to bring you information

595
00:32:35,722 --> 00:32:37,190
about the fighting.

596
00:32:37,223 --> 00:32:39,292
- We're trying
to keep a lid on this thing.

597
00:32:39,325 --> 00:32:41,560
- We've asked Dr. Timothy Breaux
to our studio tonight.

598
00:32:41,593 --> 00:32:42,527
He has seen the creatures

599
00:32:42,560 --> 00:32:44,430
and has been in consultation
with the army.

600
00:32:44,463 --> 00:32:46,165
- What it is, Dr. Breaux!

601
00:32:46,198 --> 00:32:49,502
- We have late reports
from several sources

602
00:32:49,535 --> 00:32:51,437
about these creatures.
But you saw them.

603
00:32:51,470 --> 00:32:54,473
Are they from outer space?
Are they animals? What are they?

604
00:32:54,507 --> 00:32:57,343
I believe they are
a form of animal life

605
00:32:57,377 --> 00:32:58,712
but not from outer space.

606
00:32:58,745 --> 00:33:00,480
- Then where are they from?

607
00:33:00,513 --> 00:33:03,248
- The ones I saw came from
underground out of the sewers.

608
00:33:03,282 --> 00:33:05,317
- The sewers?
- Yes.

609
00:33:05,350 --> 00:33:07,453
- But of course I'm flying
right now too.

610
00:33:07,487 --> 00:33:10,490
- ...by the authorities that
these creatures have existed

611
00:33:10,523 --> 00:33:12,225
in the subterranean areas.

612
00:33:12,258 --> 00:33:14,193
- But why have they attacked us?

613
00:33:14,227 --> 00:33:15,795
- It's mostly my fault,
actually.

614
00:33:15,828 --> 00:33:18,430
And my I say how sorry I am.

615
00:33:18,463 --> 00:33:21,700
- ...and nuclear devices.
I believe that this has

616
00:33:21,733 --> 00:33:24,237
seriously disturbed
their former homes.

617
00:33:24,270 --> 00:33:28,207
They have been forced by us
to seek shelter above ground.

618
00:33:28,240 --> 00:33:29,675
That is why we have fog.

619
00:33:29,708 --> 00:33:31,810
- But how do they make fog?

620
00:33:31,843 --> 00:33:33,712
- I really don't know.
But one thing is certain.

621
00:33:33,745 --> 00:33:36,815
The ring of fog that they have
thrown around and above the city

622
00:33:36,848 --> 00:33:40,252
is an attempt to permanently
lower our temperature

623
00:33:40,285 --> 00:33:42,655
so that they can live forever
above ground.

624
00:33:42,688 --> 00:33:45,425
- Dr. Breaux, exactly what
do these creatures look like?

625
00:33:45,458 --> 00:33:49,462
- They're large, huge...
- Big almost.

626
00:33:49,495 --> 00:33:53,432
- ...prehistoric, covered with
scales and covered with slime.

627
00:33:53,465 --> 00:33:55,100
- The slime people.

628
00:33:55,133 --> 00:33:56,402
- Well, I wouldn't say that.

629
00:33:56,435 --> 00:33:59,138
- Excuse me, doctor.
- Zsa Zsa is guilty.

630
00:33:59,172 --> 00:34:00,272
- I have a late bulletin here.

631
00:34:00,305 --> 00:34:02,542
We have a report that
a reinforcements have arrived

632
00:34:02,575 --> 00:34:04,810
to join the forces
now fighting the slime people.

633
00:34:04,843 --> 00:34:08,147
More units are on their way
across the country.

634
00:34:08,180 --> 00:34:11,150
Residents are warned
not to leave their homes.

635
00:34:11,183 --> 00:34:14,553
Repeat stay indoors
until further notice.

636
00:34:14,587 --> 00:34:15,822
- Play board games or something.

637
00:34:15,855 --> 00:34:17,823
The one with the pictures
is kind of fun.

638
00:34:17,857 --> 00:34:20,292
- ...200 feet from here.
This area has been searched

639
00:34:20,325 --> 00:34:21,793
and reported clear
of slime people.

640
00:34:21,827 --> 00:34:23,797
Although the battle still rages
north of the city.

641
00:34:23,830 --> 00:34:26,332
Casualties have been tremendous
in these two days of fighting.

642
00:34:26,365 --> 00:34:28,700
- Ah,
that's impressive casualties.

643
00:34:28,733 --> 00:34:29,567
- ...all points
of the normal fog.

644
00:34:29,600 --> 00:34:32,572
Here in the quiet area,
men are working

645
00:34:32,605 --> 00:34:34,307
trying to clear
the area of the fog.

646
00:34:34,340 --> 00:34:36,242
It is believed that if we do
clear the fog,

647
00:34:36,275 --> 00:34:37,710
the battle will be over.

648
00:34:37,743 --> 00:34:40,145
Meantime, planes are overhead
seeding the clouds

649
00:34:40,178 --> 00:34:42,548
and hoping that rain
will clear the air.

650
00:34:42,582 --> 00:34:44,550
I hope I'm still visible
in here.

651
00:34:44,583 --> 00:34:46,318
- Huh?

652
00:34:46,352 --> 00:34:49,322
- It can't be! It's hardened.
The fog has hardened.

653
00:34:49,355 --> 00:34:51,690
- Somebody get in here quick!
- Unharden that man!

654
00:34:51,723 --> 00:34:53,325
What are you trying to do,
make me look bad?

655
00:34:53,358 --> 00:34:54,760
- It's horrible.
It's thickening.

656
00:34:54,793 --> 00:34:57,630
- You saw it. There's a man
in the fog. It's hardened.

657
00:34:57,663 --> 00:34:58,763
It's a solid wall in there.

658
00:34:58,797 --> 00:35:01,267
Colonel, Colonel can you tell me
what's happening, sir?

659
00:35:01,300 --> 00:35:03,235
- Uh, yes, the fog has hardened.

660
00:35:03,268 --> 00:35:05,270
- The fog seems to have turned
to stone all around the city.

661
00:35:05,303 --> 00:35:06,738
- What about the army?
- They've hardened too.

662
00:35:06,772 --> 00:35:08,273
- They're on the other side
of the wall now.

663
00:35:08,307 --> 00:35:10,142
- They've been forced out
completely.

664
00:35:10,175 --> 00:35:13,212
Looks as if the slime people
have won for now.

665
00:35:13,245 --> 00:35:14,747
Are you on television?

666
00:35:14,780 --> 00:35:15,815
- Yes, sir. We are. Right there.

667
00:35:15,848 --> 00:35:17,717
- Well, here are official orders

668
00:35:17,750 --> 00:35:19,718
for complete evacuation
immediately.

669
00:35:19,752 --> 00:35:24,323
All civilian and military
personnel must leave the area.

670
00:35:24,357 --> 00:35:28,160
I repeat. All civilian
and military personnel

671
00:35:28,193 --> 00:35:29,395
must leave the area.

672
00:35:29,428 --> 00:35:32,332
- Elvis has left the building.

673
00:35:32,365 --> 00:35:36,768
- ...and we've set up emergency
shelters on the other side.

674
00:35:36,802 --> 00:35:40,372
Of course, we will try
to continue penetrating

675
00:35:40,405 --> 00:35:43,208
the fog wall
after everybody is out.

676
00:35:43,242 --> 00:35:44,475
- Thank you, Colonel. That's it.

677
00:35:44,508 --> 00:35:47,613
Everybody evacuate to the south
or east. Evacuate immediately.

678
00:35:47,647 --> 00:35:49,615
- Well, that's the way
it all began.

679
00:35:49,648 --> 00:35:53,920
- You have any more?
- Yeah, there's one more.

680
00:35:53,953 --> 00:35:56,355
- Aah!

681
00:35:56,388 --> 00:35:58,390
- It's Roger Ebert!

682
00:35:58,423 --> 00:36:00,927
- Look, Gene, I thought
it was shoddy and manipulative.

683
00:36:00,960 --> 00:36:03,795
A shameless tearjerker.
I give it a thumbs down.

684
00:36:03,828 --> 00:36:07,567
- I liked it, Roger. I thought
that dancing was uplifting.

685
00:36:11,270 --> 00:36:13,672
- Who are they?
- Critics.

686
00:36:13,705 --> 00:36:15,307
- Looters. After our money or--

687
00:36:15,340 --> 00:36:17,643
- Tom,
they're not our problem now.

688
00:36:17,677 --> 00:36:19,612
It's getting close
to the dew point.

689
00:36:19,645 --> 00:36:21,313
- You mean,
they'll be coming out soon?

690
00:36:21,347 --> 00:36:23,682
- Let's get to Studio One.
It's got double doors.

691
00:36:23,715 --> 00:36:26,418
- Double doors. Great big
French windows, a sauna.

692
00:36:26,452 --> 00:36:28,587
You'll love it.
Everybody step this way.

693
00:36:28,620 --> 00:36:32,758
- So do we assume now
that Vince is dead?

694
00:36:40,265 --> 00:36:41,500
- Over there!

695
00:36:43,468 --> 00:36:45,270
- Oh.

696
00:36:45,303 --> 00:36:48,573
- Ah, guys, it's just me.
You're old friend Joe.

697
00:36:48,607 --> 00:36:50,575
I have a staph infection
and eczema.

698
00:36:50,608 --> 00:36:52,845
But hey, come on guys.
Let me come with you.

699
00:36:52,878 --> 00:36:54,980
I'm not gonna kiss you,
you know.

700
00:37:06,925 --> 00:37:08,460
- Hurry!

701
00:37:11,430 --> 00:37:12,732
- Aah!

702
00:37:12,765 --> 00:37:14,700
- Fabian.

703
00:37:14,733 --> 00:37:16,768
- Am I glad to see you.

704
00:37:16,802 --> 00:37:19,872
They're coming
by the hundreds out there.

705
00:37:19,905 --> 00:37:21,873
- Tom, we don't have much time.

706
00:37:23,642 --> 00:37:26,512
They must've seen me.
Come on, down here.

707
00:37:28,947 --> 00:37:32,385
- Well, it's a broken water main
all right, Ralphie boy.

708
00:37:34,387 --> 00:37:37,490
- Like the film?

709
00:37:37,523 --> 00:37:41,727
- Okay everybody, stop.
Look scared. Move on.

710
00:37:41,760 --> 00:37:43,828
Thank you. Everybody.
Stop, good.

711
00:37:46,398 --> 00:37:49,635
- Come on, Marie, push.
There we go.

712
00:37:49,668 --> 00:37:51,770
Come on. Everybody in here now.

713
00:37:51,803 --> 00:37:54,640
- Now close it up.

714
00:37:54,673 --> 00:37:57,610
- It's moving!

715
00:37:57,643 --> 00:38:01,380
- Stop that! It tickles. Heh.

716
00:38:03,048 --> 00:38:04,583
- Help me with this door.

717
00:38:16,962 --> 00:38:20,465
- Great. Now they're
in slackhouse funf.

718
00:38:23,102 --> 00:38:25,503
- Tom, do you think
that'll hold 'em?

719
00:38:25,537 --> 00:38:28,640
- Don't worry, honey.
Just hope we keep getting power.

720
00:38:28,673 --> 00:38:30,642
- Incidentally,
my name's Calvin Johnson.

721
00:38:30,675 --> 00:38:32,110
- So what?
- Just call me Cal.

722
00:38:32,143 --> 00:38:35,680
- Hey, Cal.
My daughters Lisa and Bonnie.

723
00:38:35,713 --> 00:38:37,617
- And this is Tom Gregory.
- How are you?

724
00:38:37,650 --> 00:38:39,818
- Odds are getting mighty unfair
out there.

725
00:38:39,852 --> 00:38:41,820
Sure glad I ran into you.

726
00:38:41,853 --> 00:38:43,822
- Guess we're all lucky
to be in here.

727
00:38:43,855 --> 00:38:46,858
- There's a lot of actors
who aren't even working.

728
00:38:46,892 --> 00:38:48,460
- Come on.
- It's not much.

729
00:38:48,493 --> 00:38:49,962
But I call it hell.

730
00:38:56,468 --> 00:38:57,970
- What are you doing?

731
00:38:58,003 --> 00:39:01,440
- We left some looters behind
in the projection room.

732
00:39:01,473 --> 00:39:03,542
They're no friends of mine.
But at least we can warn them.

733
00:39:03,575 --> 00:39:06,578
- Hello no friends of mine.

734
00:39:06,612 --> 00:39:09,115
- Listen to me. If you're
still in there, look out.

735
00:39:09,148 --> 00:39:13,118
Barricade the doors.
Get the seats up against them.

736
00:39:13,152 --> 00:39:16,022
- Shut up! Shut up!

737
00:39:16,055 --> 00:39:18,090
- Oh, it's Tom Waits
on a bender.

738
00:39:28,767 --> 00:39:31,603
- Must be the new
Hi-C ecto cooler.

739
00:39:33,738 --> 00:39:37,510
- Oh, that water main is going
to cost you 500 bucks.

740
00:39:37,543 --> 00:39:41,513
- Hey, it's Michael J. Pollard.

741
00:39:44,483 --> 00:39:47,085
- Which one?
- That guy.

742
00:39:51,057 --> 00:39:52,090
- Who are you?

743
00:39:52,123 --> 00:39:53,725
- Bobcat Goldthwait.

744
00:39:53,758 --> 00:39:55,760
- Ow! Aah!

745
00:39:55,793 --> 00:39:57,730
- So much for Police Academy 8.

746
00:39:57,763 --> 00:40:00,732
- Tickle machine.
Tickle machine.

747
00:40:14,580 --> 00:40:16,448
- Professor, would you mind
sitting over there

748
00:40:16,482 --> 00:40:17,817
next to Bonnie?

749
00:40:17,850 --> 00:40:19,785
I'm going to try
to get a message out.

750
00:40:19,818 --> 00:40:21,520
- Keep your hands to yourself.
- I doubt very much

751
00:40:21,553 --> 00:40:23,955
whether anything will get
through that dome.

752
00:40:23,988 --> 00:40:25,022
- But there's no harm trying.

753
00:40:25,055 --> 00:40:28,227
Cal, swing that monitor
around this way, will you?

754
00:40:28,260 --> 00:40:30,562
Now if our picture is getting
through,

755
00:40:30,595 --> 00:40:31,830
we'll see it right there.

756
00:40:31,863 --> 00:40:33,198
- Are we going to be
on television?

757
00:40:33,232 --> 00:40:36,135
- Yes. But our Nielsen rating
won't be very high.

758
00:40:36,168 --> 00:40:38,803
- If you'll hold that card
right up to camera, okay.

759
00:40:38,837 --> 00:40:41,640
Cal, you can sit there on the
arm right next to the professor.

760
00:40:41,673 --> 00:40:42,707
- But keep your hands
to yourself.

761
00:40:42,740 --> 00:40:46,778
- Now just relax,
and I'll switch on the camera.

762
00:40:46,812 --> 00:40:49,548
- Now has everyone swallowed
their barium?

763
00:40:49,582 --> 00:40:51,617
- Let's all keep our fingers
crossed, huh?

764
00:40:51,650 --> 00:40:55,787
- Professor,
sing the theme song and...yes.

765
00:40:55,820 --> 00:40:58,857
This is Slime Witness News
with anchorperson Ernie Cofax.

766
00:41:04,263 --> 00:41:07,700
- This is KTTV Studios
in Hollywood.

767
00:41:07,733 --> 00:41:09,702
If you are receiving
this signal,

768
00:41:09,735 --> 00:41:12,938
please contact authorities
and us if at all possible.

769
00:41:12,972 --> 00:41:14,873
- There's nothing
on the monitor.

770
00:41:14,907 --> 00:41:16,208
Does that mean they
can't hear us?

771
00:41:16,242 --> 00:41:19,077
- We left the lens cap on.
- Oh!

772
00:41:19,110 --> 00:41:21,013
- You know,
there's a slight chance

773
00:41:21,047 --> 00:41:23,182
they might pick up the audio.

774
00:41:23,215 --> 00:41:25,150
I'll give it a whirl anyway.

775
00:41:25,183 --> 00:41:28,720
This is KTTV Studios in
Hollywood to Mt. Wilson.

776
00:41:28,753 --> 00:41:31,657
We are being attacked
by the slime people.

777
00:41:31,690 --> 00:41:34,125
- Send reams of Kleenex.

778
00:41:34,158 --> 00:41:36,695
- If you have any information
about this wall,

779
00:41:36,728 --> 00:41:38,563
please contact us immediately.

780
00:41:38,597 --> 00:41:39,832
- Or Pink Floyd.

781
00:41:39,865 --> 00:41:41,267
- If anybody's listening,
this is no joke.

782
00:41:41,300 --> 00:41:42,902
I'm a Marine.

783
00:41:42,935 --> 00:41:45,270
- I'd like to sing a melody
to the King's greatest hits.

784
00:41:45,303 --> 00:41:47,873
- I guess they thought
I was dead and left me there.

785
00:41:47,907 --> 00:41:50,608
The slime people made a fog.
And the fog...

786
00:41:50,642 --> 00:41:53,612
- Boy,
I hate these pledge drives.

787
00:41:53,645 --> 00:41:55,613
- ...knows how
to get through this thing,

788
00:41:55,647 --> 00:41:58,650
then I'm sure that there's a few
other people just like us...

789
00:41:58,683 --> 00:42:01,320
- I spent a year working with
these special individuals

790
00:42:01,353 --> 00:42:04,188
and seen more love come
out of their tiny hearts.

791
00:42:04,222 --> 00:42:05,925
- Do you think anybody heard it?
- Aah!

792
00:42:05,958 --> 00:42:07,292
- Huh?
- What's wrong?

793
00:42:07,325 --> 00:42:09,728
We're losing power fast.

794
00:42:09,762 --> 00:42:11,897
- Where's he going, Dad?

795
00:42:11,930 --> 00:42:14,232
- I think he's trying
to conserve electricity.

796
00:42:14,265 --> 00:42:16,202
- Hmm.

797
00:42:16,235 --> 00:42:19,738
- There we go. I just
rewired the entire studio.

798
00:42:19,772 --> 00:42:22,573
- That'll last us through
the night, I hope.

799
00:42:22,607 --> 00:42:24,310
- It's dead. Nothing.

800
00:42:24,343 --> 00:42:26,045
- I was hoping we could find out

801
00:42:26,078 --> 00:42:27,880
what was going on outside
the dome.

802
00:42:27,913 --> 00:42:29,782
- Maybe Cal can help us.

803
00:42:29,815 --> 00:42:31,750
Cal, did you actually
see the wall?

804
00:42:31,783 --> 00:42:34,653
- No, I saw them on the dark
side of the moon tour.

805
00:42:34,687 --> 00:42:36,022
- One minute there was just
a fog,

806
00:42:36,055 --> 00:42:38,223
the next got thick and hard.

807
00:42:38,257 --> 00:42:40,358
- Did you touch it?
Was it hot or cold?

808
00:42:40,392 --> 00:42:41,592
Or rough or smooth? Or what?

809
00:42:41,625 --> 00:42:45,130
- I didn't get close enough.
The slime men were coming back.

810
00:42:45,163 --> 00:42:47,598
And all I saw was...

811
00:42:47,632 --> 00:42:51,237
- Oh, heck,
I disgraced myself like crazy.

812
00:42:51,270 --> 00:42:54,340
- ...coming in all around.
I didn't see anything else.

813
00:42:54,373 --> 00:42:57,677
That's when I came back here.
I hid all last night.

814
00:42:57,710 --> 00:42:58,977
Spent a day trying to find out

815
00:42:59,010 --> 00:43:00,345
if there was anybody else
besides me

816
00:43:00,378 --> 00:43:02,682
that was alive in here.

817
00:43:02,715 --> 00:43:04,917
- Was there besides us?

818
00:43:04,950 --> 00:43:06,285
- I ran across some looters

819
00:43:06,318 --> 00:43:09,153
and a couple of people going
crazy trying to get out.

820
00:43:09,187 --> 00:43:13,692
And some that was so scared
they wouldn't even talk to me.

821
00:43:13,725 --> 00:43:15,093
- Anyone else?

822
00:43:15,127 --> 00:43:17,195
- Not alive.

823
00:43:17,228 --> 00:43:21,100
- Well, I suggest that we try
to get some rest.

824
00:43:21,133 --> 00:43:24,202
Tomorrow, Cal can take us
to where he first saw the wall.

825
00:43:24,235 --> 00:43:25,202
- Berlin?

826
00:43:25,235 --> 00:43:27,740
- We can hold up every chemical
we possibly can

827
00:43:27,773 --> 00:43:29,207
to try and see
what penetrates it.

828
00:43:29,240 --> 00:43:31,643
- There ought to be one.
- Ought to be?

829
00:43:31,677 --> 00:43:32,978
Professor, there's got to be.

830
00:43:33,012 --> 00:43:34,178
- Should be.
- I'll take first watch.

831
00:43:34,212 --> 00:43:35,713
- There you are, Cal.

832
00:43:35,747 --> 00:43:39,183
- That's not a watch.
That's a gun.

833
00:43:39,217 --> 00:43:42,855
- This is how I keep time,
city boy.

834
00:43:48,327 --> 00:43:49,762
- Cal? Cal?

835
00:43:49,795 --> 00:43:51,897
- Do you have a hall pass,
Bonnie?

836
00:43:51,930 --> 00:43:53,065
I'll have to shoot you
if you don't.

837
00:43:53,098 --> 00:43:55,867
Why aren't you sleeping
like the others?

838
00:43:55,900 --> 00:43:57,368
- I couldn't sleep.

839
00:43:57,402 --> 00:44:00,272
Knowing those things are
out there gives me the creeps.

840
00:44:00,305 --> 00:44:02,673
- I know. But I'll warn you
in plenty of time

841
00:44:02,707 --> 00:44:04,008
if anything happens.

842
00:44:04,042 --> 00:44:05,410
Now you go on back
with the others.

843
00:44:05,443 --> 00:44:07,112
We're going to have
a big day tomorrow.

844
00:44:07,145 --> 00:44:10,148
I'd rather stay with you.

845
00:44:10,182 --> 00:44:11,750
Don't you want me to?

846
00:44:11,783 --> 00:44:14,753
- Sure. But--

847
00:44:14,787 --> 00:44:16,020
- Well, then it's all settled.

848
00:44:16,053 --> 00:44:18,790
- There is something
I wanted to ask you.

849
00:44:18,823 --> 00:44:21,327
Did you have a boyfriend
before all this happened?

850
00:44:21,360 --> 00:44:23,395
- Only barrack 14.
- I had lots of dates.

851
00:44:23,428 --> 00:44:25,197
But there was no one special.

852
00:44:25,230 --> 00:44:27,733
You know, just Elvis and,
oh, Jerry Lee.

853
00:44:30,502 --> 00:44:32,403
- Do you care?

854
00:44:32,437 --> 00:44:35,173
- Not at all.
That's why I'm talking to you.

855
00:44:35,207 --> 00:44:37,910
- I was just wondering.

856
00:44:37,943 --> 00:44:39,412
Your dad was acting
kind of funny.

857
00:44:39,445 --> 00:44:41,880
Did he care that I came along?

858
00:44:41,913 --> 00:44:45,483
- He's just worried about
getting us out of here.

859
00:44:45,517 --> 00:44:48,387
I'm sure he didn't mind.

860
00:44:48,420 --> 00:44:51,757
Wouldn't have left anybody
out there.

861
00:44:51,790 --> 00:44:52,992
- Am I just anybody?

862
00:44:53,025 --> 00:44:56,495
- I don't think so.
- You're nobody.

863
00:44:56,528 --> 00:44:58,297
- You're so brave.

864
00:44:58,330 --> 00:45:00,465
I mean, fighting and everything.

865
00:45:00,498 --> 00:45:03,402
- I didn't do any more than
what my buddies would've done.

866
00:45:03,435 --> 00:45:05,137
I don't think so.

867
00:45:08,273 --> 00:45:10,275
- Bonnie,
do you use heavy makeup

868
00:45:10,308 --> 00:45:13,145
to cover up your mustache?

869
00:45:13,178 --> 00:45:15,380
- Bonnie...

870
00:45:15,413 --> 00:45:18,317
- Can you field strip an M-16?

871
00:45:18,350 --> 00:45:22,087
- Mmm. You taste like root beer
barrels.

872
00:45:22,120 --> 00:45:24,288
- Maybe I shouldn't
have done that.

873
00:45:24,322 --> 00:45:25,723
- Give me back my gum!

874
00:45:27,392 --> 00:45:30,795
- I don't know.
I think I kind of liked it.

875
00:45:30,828 --> 00:45:32,763
- Hey, Mikey, she likes it.

876
00:45:32,797 --> 00:45:34,800
- I'm not the kind of guy

877
00:45:34,833 --> 00:45:36,935
that just goes around
kissing any girl.

878
00:45:36,968 --> 00:45:39,838
- Well,
just any girl that says yes.

879
00:45:39,872 --> 00:45:43,775
- I'm glad.
Because when we get out of here,

880
00:45:43,808 --> 00:45:45,143
I want to see a lot more of you.

881
00:45:45,177 --> 00:45:47,980
Right now I've got a job to do.

882
00:45:48,013 --> 00:45:49,080
- I know.

883
00:45:49,113 --> 00:45:50,282
- Gee whiz.

884
00:45:50,315 --> 00:45:53,152
You know as long as
you're sittin' here,

885
00:45:53,185 --> 00:45:55,253
I don't even want
to think about slime people.

886
00:45:55,287 --> 00:45:57,923
Although it's hard looking
at a mug like yours.

887
00:45:57,957 --> 00:46:01,058
You'd better get out of here.
right now, you hear?

888
00:46:01,092 --> 00:46:03,328
- A Marine that says "gee whiz."

889
00:46:03,362 --> 00:46:06,865
What's he going to do?
Storm the Cunningham house?

890
00:46:06,898 --> 00:46:10,068
- Bonnie...

891
00:46:10,102 --> 00:46:12,270
goodnight.

892
00:46:12,303 --> 00:46:14,873
- Goodnight.

893
00:46:14,907 --> 00:46:15,907
- Young love.

894
00:46:23,048 --> 00:46:26,818
- Hey come everybody, get up.
You're making union scale.

895
00:46:26,852 --> 00:46:29,287
What do you want? Wake up.

896
00:46:29,320 --> 00:46:32,290
- Uh, excuse me, Mr. Mailman.

897
00:46:32,323 --> 00:46:35,427
Uh, Mr. Letterman's looking
for you.

898
00:46:35,460 --> 00:46:38,897
There's no sign of them
anywhere outside.

899
00:46:38,930 --> 00:46:43,902
- Oh, is it cooler
than yesterday?

900
00:46:43,935 --> 00:46:47,038
- I think it is.
Does that mean--

901
00:46:47,072 --> 00:46:48,573
- Oh, no.
- Yep.

902
00:46:48,607 --> 00:46:50,408
- I hate when they do that.

903
00:46:50,442 --> 00:46:51,910
- They're trying to bring
our temperature down

904
00:46:51,943 --> 00:46:53,412
to a constant dew point.

905
00:46:53,445 --> 00:46:56,915
That way they'll be able
to circulate on the surface

906
00:46:56,948 --> 00:46:58,450
both day and night.

907
00:46:58,483 --> 00:47:00,485
- I, uh--I checked
your station wagon.

908
00:47:00,518 --> 00:47:02,453
It hasn't been touched.

909
00:47:02,487 --> 00:47:04,590
- My car's there too.
- Good.

910
00:47:04,623 --> 00:47:06,325
- Hear them run.

911
00:47:06,358 --> 00:47:07,927
- All right, come on, my
sleeping beauties.

912
00:47:07,960 --> 00:47:09,227
Up and at 'em.

913
00:47:09,260 --> 00:47:11,630
- Here we go.
- Good morning, Tom.

914
00:47:11,663 --> 00:47:13,032
- Morning.

915
00:47:13,065 --> 00:47:14,432
- What do you suggest we do now,
sir?

916
00:47:14,465 --> 00:47:16,468
- Let's twist.

917
00:47:16,502 --> 00:47:19,938
- ...we've got to break
that wall and get through

918
00:47:19,972 --> 00:47:22,073
in a matter of a few hours.

919
00:47:22,107 --> 00:47:25,210
- Then we can shower up
and make the party, Dad.

920
00:47:25,243 --> 00:47:26,545
- ...list of things here
that we'll need.

921
00:47:26,578 --> 00:47:28,513
This plus the chemicals

922
00:47:28,547 --> 00:47:30,448
that I'll get from the
laboratory will give us a start.

923
00:47:30,482 --> 00:47:33,118
- An awful lot, Dad.

924
00:47:33,152 --> 00:47:34,385
- It's an awful lot, Dad.

925
00:47:34,418 --> 00:47:36,888
- We've got mine too.
It's in the garage.

926
00:47:36,922 --> 00:47:38,957
- I suggest that we divide up.

927
00:47:38,990 --> 00:47:42,260
That'll give us a chance to get
this shopping done much quicker.

928
00:47:42,293 --> 00:47:43,395
- Good idea or looting.

929
00:47:43,428 --> 00:47:45,163
- Cal, you take
my station wagon.

930
00:47:45,197 --> 00:47:46,263
- He'd rather have the girl.

931
00:47:46,297 --> 00:47:49,567
Find the nearest surplus store
and get these items.

932
00:47:49,600 --> 00:47:50,600
- Yes, sir.

933
00:47:50,633 --> 00:47:52,003
- He's going to need help
with all that, Dad.

934
00:47:52,037 --> 00:47:53,070
I'll go with him.

935
00:47:53,103 --> 00:47:54,372
- All right, Bonnie.
Take good care of her, Cal.

936
00:47:54,405 --> 00:47:56,007
- I will, sir. I promise.

937
00:47:56,040 --> 00:47:58,643
- That's what he's afraid of.

938
00:47:58,677 --> 00:48:00,345
- Come on, honey. Here we go.

939
00:48:00,378 --> 00:48:02,047
Professor.

940
00:48:07,585 --> 00:48:11,590
- ♪ 99 bottles of beer
on the wall ♪

941
00:48:13,258 --> 00:48:16,227
- And remember, folks,
even at the end of the world,

942
00:48:16,260 --> 00:48:18,530
signal those turns.

943
00:48:18,563 --> 00:48:22,367
- Such dramatic driving music.

944
00:48:34,278 --> 00:48:37,182
- Hey, nice cups.

945
00:48:40,318 --> 00:48:44,522
- You see, Tom, this wall that
they've thrown around the city

946
00:48:44,555 --> 00:48:48,027
is evidently some sort of
dome-like affair.

947
00:48:48,060 --> 00:48:50,428
Otherwise, we would have
the military planes

948
00:48:50,462 --> 00:48:51,563
bombing us from above.

949
00:48:51,597 --> 00:48:53,098
- Have you any idea
how they make it?

950
00:48:53,132 --> 00:48:55,200
- It's probably
subcontracted out.

951
00:48:55,233 --> 00:48:58,303
- With some sort of machine
or mechanism.

952
00:48:58,337 --> 00:49:00,538
They probably inject
some element

953
00:49:00,572 --> 00:49:02,107
into a controlled air mass.

954
00:49:02,140 --> 00:49:04,375
- Like heroine.
- This combines chemically

955
00:49:04,408 --> 00:49:06,177
with an element
in our atmosphere.

956
00:49:06,210 --> 00:49:08,047
Probably nitrogen.

957
00:49:08,080 --> 00:49:10,548
Thereby forming
a solid compound.

958
00:49:10,582 --> 00:49:14,152
Oh, Lisa, you ought to pack
enough food supplies

959
00:49:14,185 --> 00:49:15,220
for a couple of days.

960
00:49:15,253 --> 00:49:16,187
- Sure, Mr. Hefner.

961
00:49:16,220 --> 00:49:19,257
- Poor little Bonnie must just
be famished.

962
00:49:19,290 --> 00:49:22,760
- Yeah,
I'll get busy in the lab.

963
00:49:22,793 --> 00:49:26,665
- And we'll get busy
in the kitchen. Heh heh.

964
00:49:28,233 --> 00:49:30,468
- How about me making us
some coffee?

965
00:49:30,502 --> 00:49:33,438
- Where is it?
- Right here.

966
00:49:33,472 --> 00:49:35,740
- Man, I don't know about you,
Professor,

967
00:49:35,773 --> 00:49:38,710
but I got a thing about women
on stepladders.

968
00:49:38,743 --> 00:49:40,778
- ...you're quite a gal.

969
00:49:40,812 --> 00:49:43,382
- As someone who's trying to get
us out of all this,

970
00:49:43,415 --> 00:49:45,683
I think you should have more
important things on your mind.

971
00:49:45,717 --> 00:49:47,552
- Love coffee.

972
00:49:47,585 --> 00:49:51,355
- Awful lot.

973
00:49:51,388 --> 00:49:54,692
- Oh, tasting my daughter,
I see.

974
00:49:54,725 --> 00:49:57,528
Well, I'll just stare
at these curtains.

975
00:49:57,562 --> 00:49:59,630
- Penetrating that wall
may prove to be

976
00:49:59,663 --> 00:50:01,600
a very valuable experiment.

977
00:50:01,633 --> 00:50:04,302
If anything like this ever
happens again.

978
00:50:04,335 --> 00:50:07,438
- We got to go, you guys.

979
00:50:07,472 --> 00:50:08,473
- It was just getting
interesting.

980
00:50:08,507 --> 00:50:11,075
- Tell me about it.

981
00:50:11,108 --> 00:50:12,277
- You know what it's like being
the daughter of a scientist.

982
00:50:12,310 --> 00:50:15,013
- It's not dull.
Here, let me help you.

983
00:50:21,085 --> 00:50:23,355
- Joel, I don't understand
why this movie was made.

984
00:50:23,388 --> 00:50:24,622
- What's that, Crow? Why?

985
00:50:24,655 --> 00:50:27,792
- I just don't understand why
someone would spend so much time

986
00:50:27,825 --> 00:50:29,560
trying to make a movie
that was obviously

987
00:50:29,593 --> 00:50:31,162
conjured up over lunch.

988
00:50:31,195 --> 00:50:33,265
- Yeah, it is sort of
a loose end festival.

989
00:50:33,298 --> 00:50:35,067
Like,
what are these slime people?

990
00:50:35,100 --> 00:50:36,300
They can't really be reptiles,

991
00:50:36,333 --> 00:50:38,137
Because reptiles need heat
to live.

992
00:50:38,170 --> 00:50:39,637
And they're trying to make it
cold on the planet.

993
00:50:39,670 --> 00:50:41,573
- Yeah. And how did they
become slime people

994
00:50:41,607 --> 00:50:43,308
living in the center
of the earth and all.

995
00:50:43,342 --> 00:50:44,608
As far as I know,

996
00:50:44,642 --> 00:50:46,545
slime isn't exactly a naturally
occurring element

997
00:50:46,578 --> 00:50:48,313
even in the bowels of the earth.

998
00:50:48,347 --> 00:50:50,148
- Yeah, I think it would've been
much more believable

999
00:50:50,182 --> 00:50:52,483
if they would've made them
monsters invading from space.

1000
00:50:52,517 --> 00:50:54,152
You know, then at least
they could play by rules

1001
00:50:54,185 --> 00:50:55,387
we can't even comprehend.

1002
00:50:55,420 --> 00:50:57,155
- Well, the thing is, you guys,

1003
00:50:57,188 --> 00:50:58,590
the real beauty part
of this movie is that

1004
00:50:58,623 --> 00:51:00,092
it actually got made.

1005
00:51:00,125 --> 00:51:02,293
You know, the guy who made it
isn't a fool.

1006
00:51:02,327 --> 00:51:04,863
He just convinced some people
that it was worth making.

1007
00:51:04,897 --> 00:51:07,832
You know, whether it was a good
idea or it could make money

1008
00:51:07,865 --> 00:51:10,802
or it satisfied some bizarre
urge in the viewing public.

1009
00:51:10,835 --> 00:51:13,472
- Oh, I see. So it's
the gullibility of humans

1010
00:51:13,505 --> 00:51:15,373
that allows things like this
to exist.

1011
00:51:15,407 --> 00:51:16,607
- I guess you're right. Yeah.

1012
00:51:16,640 --> 00:51:19,443
- So I could make a movie about
a giant apple sauce monster

1013
00:51:19,477 --> 00:51:21,345
who goes around smooshing up
people's food

1014
00:51:21,378 --> 00:51:23,215
until there's nothing left
to chew on the planet.

1015
00:51:23,248 --> 00:51:26,217
- Provided you could get
someone to pay for it,

1016
00:51:26,250 --> 00:51:27,685
think it was a good idea
and worth making.

1017
00:51:27,718 --> 00:51:29,553
- Oh, well, I have a good idea
for a show then.

1018
00:51:29,587 --> 00:51:30,587
I'm sure it'll work.

1019
00:51:30,620 --> 00:51:32,857
Okay. This guy gets stranded
on a desert isle.

1020
00:51:32,890 --> 00:51:35,727
He makes little mechanical
friends from parts of his boat.

1021
00:51:35,760 --> 00:51:36,593
Then all day long they have

1022
00:51:36,627 --> 00:51:39,463
to sit and watch reruns
of bad TV shows,

1023
00:51:39,497 --> 00:51:42,233
like Super Train
or Greatest American Hero.

1024
00:51:42,267 --> 00:51:44,668
- Oh, no, what would they eat?

1025
00:51:44,702 --> 00:51:46,538
- It doesn't matter.
You should just relax.

1026
00:51:46,572 --> 00:51:47,838
It's just a show.

1027
00:51:47,872 --> 00:51:51,442
- Well, why would they be
watching the shows then?

1028
00:51:51,475 --> 00:51:53,478
- Uh, how about
if two evil commodores

1029
00:51:53,512 --> 00:51:55,280
send the shows to him?

1030
00:51:55,313 --> 00:51:56,882
- Yeah!
- Uh, I don't think so.

1031
00:51:56,915 --> 00:51:58,750
It's very illogical.
Wouldn't fly.

1032
00:51:58,783 --> 00:51:59,817
- Really?
- Nope.

1033
00:51:59,850 --> 00:52:01,218
- Too far fetched.
- Yep.

1034
00:52:01,252 --> 00:52:03,355
- Well, let's got think
of some more, Servo.

1035
00:52:03,388 --> 00:52:04,822
Maybe we could get rich.

1036
00:52:04,855 --> 00:52:06,692
- Yeah.

1037
00:52:06,725 --> 00:52:09,660
- You know, you give life
to two inanimate objects

1038
00:52:09,693 --> 00:52:12,630
and they turn around
and mock your entire species.

1039
00:52:12,663 --> 00:52:14,832
You know, if I'm ever trapped
in outer space again,

1040
00:52:14,865 --> 00:52:16,433
I think I'm just going to--

1041
00:52:16,467 --> 00:52:17,502
[Alarm sounds]

1042
00:52:17,535 --> 00:52:21,172
- Oh, we got movie time!
Oh-ho

1043
00:52:27,712 --> 00:52:29,447
- We did our best, sir.

1044
00:52:29,480 --> 00:52:31,382
We ran into a gang of people
looting store.

1045
00:52:31,415 --> 00:52:34,252
They were getting too close
so we had to leave.

1046
00:52:34,285 --> 00:52:35,353
- What now, sir?
- To the wall.

1047
00:52:35,387 --> 00:52:37,222
We surveyed it from our house.

1048
00:52:37,255 --> 00:52:40,425
- Tom! Tom Gregory, old chap.

1049
00:52:40,458 --> 00:52:42,827
- Tom? Who's that?

1050
00:52:42,860 --> 00:52:44,262
- The goat man.

1051
00:52:44,295 --> 00:52:46,197
- Toliver. He's a great writer.

1052
00:52:46,230 --> 00:52:48,433
But the biggest troublemaker
I've ever known.

1053
00:52:48,467 --> 00:52:49,902
Besides, I think
he's a potential psycho.

1054
00:52:49,935 --> 00:52:51,670
- Hi, I'm goat man.

1055
00:52:53,905 --> 00:52:54,872
- Kitty.

1056
00:52:54,905 --> 00:52:58,310
- Say, are you driving
this old, worn out heap?

1057
00:52:58,343 --> 00:53:00,812
It goes, Toliver.

1058
00:53:00,845 --> 00:53:02,980
- Well, I suppose
it'll have to do.

1059
00:53:03,013 --> 00:53:04,280
Would you mind assisting me

1060
00:53:04,313 --> 00:53:07,952
with the only faithful
old friend I have left
in this world?

1061
00:53:07,985 --> 00:53:09,988
- Sure, where is he?

1062
00:53:10,022 --> 00:53:11,757
- Toliver, have you heard
of the slime people?

1063
00:53:11,790 --> 00:53:14,960
- No, but if you hum a few bars,
I could pick it up.

1064
00:53:14,993 --> 00:53:16,962
It's all a lot of nonsense.
I've been right over there

1065
00:53:16,995 --> 00:53:19,330
in my cabin all the time.
And I haven't seen a one of 'em.

1066
00:53:19,363 --> 00:53:22,800
Although I would like to,
if they exist.

1067
00:53:22,833 --> 00:53:26,303
I've decided to do a book
exposing all the insanity

1068
00:53:26,337 --> 00:53:28,607
that's going on around here.

1069
00:53:28,640 --> 00:53:31,342
- I call it the dictionary.

1070
00:53:31,375 --> 00:53:33,512
- That might work better if you
just made it into a coat

1071
00:53:33,545 --> 00:53:35,580
rather than just carrying
it around all the time.

1072
00:53:35,613 --> 00:53:37,815
- ...to run. These fools!

1073
00:53:37,848 --> 00:53:40,018
- Don't point that goat at me.
It might go off.

1074
00:53:40,052 --> 00:53:43,322
- Evacuation.
- Sounds painful.

1075
00:53:43,355 --> 00:53:46,423
If there are any beings,

1076
00:53:46,457 --> 00:53:47,958
they're probably
as harmless as ants.

1077
00:53:47,992 --> 00:53:50,862
- Obviously, he didn't see
the movie Them.

1078
00:53:50,895 --> 00:53:52,363
- Don't leave him, Tom.

1079
00:53:52,397 --> 00:53:54,665
- Oh, all right,
you lovable old coot.

1080
00:53:54,698 --> 00:53:57,668
You little subplot, come on.

1081
00:53:57,702 --> 00:54:00,305
- I'll show you all the slime
people you want to see, get in.

1082
00:54:00,338 --> 00:54:03,975
- I'll go along with you.
If you're going downtown,

1083
00:54:04,008 --> 00:54:06,610
I can gather some material.

1084
00:54:06,643 --> 00:54:09,813
By the way,
please give me my little love.

1085
00:54:09,847 --> 00:54:11,883
- No, no.
I'm afraid he's not on the list.

1086
00:54:11,917 --> 00:54:13,352
- Run along, Pan.

1087
00:54:13,385 --> 00:54:15,487
- We're not brining
your magazines either.

1088
00:54:18,490 --> 00:54:21,893
- [Sniffs] Whoo!
You work around circus animals?

1089
00:54:21,927 --> 00:54:23,862
Whoo.

1090
00:54:23,895 --> 00:54:26,765
- When cars ruled the earth.

1091
00:54:35,973 --> 00:54:38,443
- Looks like it's a radiator
problem to me.

1092
00:54:38,477 --> 00:54:41,412
- Well, here we are
at the Shriners' picnic.

1093
00:54:46,083 --> 00:54:48,787
- That fog is thicker than
Grandma's mustache.

1094
00:54:48,820 --> 00:54:51,790
- Oh, Tom, there it is.

1095
00:54:51,823 --> 00:54:54,392
Somewhere in there is the wall.

1096
00:54:54,425 --> 00:54:57,395
- All right, Cal,
you stay here with the girls.

1097
00:54:57,428 --> 00:54:59,997
Tom, you bring the ropes
and the weapons,

1098
00:55:00,030 --> 00:55:02,933
and we'll go have a look at what
our friends have built for us.

1099
00:55:04,735 --> 00:55:07,472
- Goat man will be bait.
- ...the wall.

1100
00:55:10,108 --> 00:55:13,378
Well, there's six degrees to go.

1101
00:55:13,412 --> 00:55:16,447
Bonnie, you watch your meter.

1102
00:55:16,480 --> 00:55:19,817
When it reaches dew point,
have Cal fire a shot.

1103
00:55:19,850 --> 00:55:23,087
Now we'll give two tugs on the
rope if we run into any trouble.

1104
00:55:23,120 --> 00:55:26,090
- Better take this.
- Thanks.

1105
00:55:26,123 --> 00:55:28,527
- The money?

1106
00:55:28,560 --> 00:55:29,960
- ...they'll be in there?

1107
00:55:29,993 --> 00:55:32,030
- We're only going
about 300 feet.

1108
00:55:32,063 --> 00:55:35,667
Just enough to find the wall.
All right? Let's go.

1109
00:55:35,700 --> 00:55:37,902
- If you drag out our rotting,
broken carcasses,

1110
00:55:37,935 --> 00:55:40,038
it means it didn't really work
out very well.

1111
00:55:55,420 --> 00:55:57,822
- Breathe all right?
- Oh, excuse me.

1112
00:55:57,855 --> 00:56:00,023
- Well, it's very interesting.

1113
00:56:00,057 --> 00:56:03,595
- Atmosphere seems normal.
No lack of oxygen.

1114
00:56:03,628 --> 00:56:05,730
No trace of poison gas.

1115
00:56:05,763 --> 00:56:07,965
Keep an eye out
for any movement though.

1116
00:56:07,998 --> 00:56:09,433
- Maybe there is no wall.

1117
00:56:09,467 --> 00:56:12,103
Maybe it's sort of
a high pressure dip area.

1118
00:56:12,137 --> 00:56:14,638
- It's only two degrees above
where Dad made the line, Cal.

1119
00:56:14,672 --> 00:56:16,573
- Wow, you're easily pleased.

1120
00:56:19,577 --> 00:56:23,047
- Hey, Joel, are they
in the Smoky Mountains?

1121
00:56:23,080 --> 00:56:24,182
- No.

1122
00:56:24,215 --> 00:56:26,517
- On top of old smoky?
- No.

1123
00:56:26,550 --> 00:56:29,520
- Smack him, Joel.
- No, I wouldn't do that.

1124
00:56:29,553 --> 00:56:31,622
- Yeah, you would.
- Professor.

1125
00:56:31,655 --> 00:56:32,857
Come here.

1126
00:56:35,827 --> 00:56:37,495
I thought I saw something
move down there.

1127
00:56:37,528 --> 00:56:41,432
- It's nothing. Just my heart
beating for you, Tom.

1128
00:56:41,465 --> 00:56:44,468
- I can't see anything.
Let's move on.

1129
00:56:53,612 --> 00:56:56,647
- It's the thing from
The Fantastic Four.

1130
00:56:56,680 --> 00:56:58,583
- No, that's Arty Johnson.
- Oh.

1131
00:56:58,617 --> 00:57:02,520
- I love the smell of napalm
in the morning.

1132
00:57:02,553 --> 00:57:05,157
Smells like...slime.

1133
00:57:08,693 --> 00:57:12,830
- Lovely. Lovely.

1134
00:57:23,273 --> 00:57:25,577
- It jerked a few times.

1135
00:57:25,610 --> 00:57:27,745
- Well, lead him on.
- Now it's pulling away.

1136
00:57:27,778 --> 00:57:29,147
- Keep your rod tip high.

1137
00:57:29,180 --> 00:57:30,113
- I'm gonna go and see
what happened.

1138
00:57:30,147 --> 00:57:33,685
- Set the hook!
- Dad told you to stay here!

1139
00:57:33,718 --> 00:57:36,120
- Tom is probably in there
falling over his own feet,

1140
00:57:36,153 --> 00:57:38,690
lying inundated
in his own blood.

1141
00:57:38,723 --> 00:57:40,958
- Shut up.

1142
00:57:40,992 --> 00:57:44,962
- Wait a minute, Lisa!
- Lisa! Bonnie!

1143
00:57:44,995 --> 00:57:47,598
Take this, Toliver.

1144
00:57:47,632 --> 00:57:50,000
- Hey, thanks for the twine,
cutey pie.

1145
00:57:50,033 --> 00:57:51,468
- Magic rope.

1146
00:58:02,613 --> 00:58:06,117
Oh, what a time to lose
your contact.

1147
00:58:06,150 --> 00:58:09,087
Now everybody's going
to have to look for it.

1148
00:58:15,058 --> 00:58:18,162
- Here, Tom, this is their wall.

1149
00:58:18,195 --> 00:58:20,732
- What? Are we walking through
a music video?

1150
00:58:23,333 --> 00:58:26,703
- Now, Tom, will you open up
those chemicals, please?

1151
00:58:32,210 --> 00:58:34,212
- This is Curt Gaudi.

1152
00:58:34,245 --> 00:58:36,313
This week
on the American Sportsman

1153
00:58:36,347 --> 00:58:40,083
we're hunting for slime people
with Forest Kelly.

1154
00:58:40,117 --> 00:58:42,820
- Silicon. Great for caulking
around windows

1155
00:58:42,853 --> 00:58:44,955
and some cosmetic uses as well.

1156
00:58:44,988 --> 00:58:49,060
- Try the potassium.
It's marked with K.

1157
00:58:49,093 --> 00:58:50,562
- There you are.

1158
00:58:50,595 --> 00:58:51,930
- And it's tasty.

1159
00:58:54,698 --> 00:58:58,903
- Sure, let the girl go first.
Real nice, you guys.

1160
00:58:58,937 --> 00:59:00,070
- Careful, Tom,
something's coming.

1161
00:59:00,103 --> 00:59:01,238
That's your department.

1162
00:59:01,272 --> 00:59:03,007
- You're in charge
of human resources.

1163
00:59:07,778 --> 00:59:11,882
- It's like
a mime training course.

1164
00:59:11,915 --> 00:59:14,218
- Tom, are you all right?

1165
00:59:14,252 --> 00:59:15,787
- What the devil
are you doing here?

1166
00:59:15,820 --> 00:59:18,222
- What happened?
- Bonnie, what's wrong?

1167
00:59:18,255 --> 00:59:20,292
- I thought you signaled
you were in trouble, sir.

1168
00:59:20,325 --> 00:59:23,862
- Well,
we might as well start back.

1169
00:59:23,895 --> 00:59:25,128
Nothing else we have to use.

1170
00:59:25,162 --> 00:59:28,065
There are thousands of chemicals
that we haven't used, of course.

1171
00:59:28,098 --> 00:59:31,002
Must be something that'll
penetrate that wall. But what?

1172
00:59:31,035 --> 00:59:32,970
- Maybe a hair pin?

1173
00:59:33,003 --> 00:59:34,272
- Right there, dear.

1174
00:59:38,242 --> 00:59:42,113
- Aah! Dad! Dad!

1175
00:59:46,117 --> 00:59:47,952
- Let's get out of here.

1176
00:59:47,985 --> 00:59:49,987
- Good idea.

1177
00:59:54,125 --> 00:59:55,860
- You know, I can't tell,

1178
00:59:55,893 --> 00:59:59,330
but I bet it's really action
packed under all this fog.

1179
01:00:01,265 --> 01:00:04,768
- Hey,
he's doing interpretive dance.

1180
01:00:25,222 --> 01:00:27,958
- Looks like a kindergarten
field trip.

1181
01:00:27,992 --> 01:00:29,993
- Yeah, to Scab World.

1182
01:00:35,032 --> 01:00:39,137
- Real scary being chased
by a giant crawdad.

1183
01:00:39,170 --> 01:00:42,207
You're on your own,
Cayenne Pepper. Whoo-whee.

1184
01:00:42,240 --> 01:00:43,875
Slime people.

1185
01:00:43,908 --> 01:00:46,143
- That thing they're hanging
onto must be the plot line.

1186
01:00:54,385 --> 01:00:56,787
- Those guys are slime.

1187
01:01:08,198 --> 01:01:12,337
- All right, everybody in.
Field trip's over.

1188
01:01:12,370 --> 01:01:15,272
Turn the defroster on.

1189
01:01:18,042 --> 01:01:21,078
- What's everyone want?
Burgers? Chicken?

1190
01:01:21,112 --> 01:01:24,115
[Tires screech]

1191
01:01:24,148 --> 01:01:25,750
- Has he got a license?

1192
01:01:29,187 --> 01:01:32,790
Hey, kid, what do you think
you're doing, flying a jet?

1193
01:01:32,823 --> 01:01:35,325
- Do you realize
the situation, sir?

1194
01:01:35,358 --> 01:01:38,095
- Look, Professor,
I've been through a war.

1195
01:01:38,128 --> 01:01:41,065
I've seen people panic
and run 100 times.

1196
01:01:41,098 --> 01:01:43,000
Maybe 10 times
they had a reason.

1197
01:01:43,033 --> 01:01:44,802
The other 90,
they were just stupid.

1198
01:01:44,835 --> 01:01:46,937
Crazed with idiotic fear.

1199
01:01:46,970 --> 01:01:48,472
Whichever is the case here,

1200
01:01:48,505 --> 01:01:50,775
it would still make
a darn good book.

1201
01:01:50,808 --> 01:01:52,777
- Shut up.

1202
01:01:52,810 --> 01:01:54,245
- I'd put you in it too,
Professor.

1203
01:01:54,278 --> 01:01:58,148
Hey, kid,
drop me off anywhere here.

1204
01:01:58,182 --> 01:02:00,383
I've got a hike to jack
the beach combers.

1205
01:02:00,417 --> 01:02:03,387
I feel an inspiration
to start right in.

1206
01:02:03,420 --> 01:02:07,392
I'll--I'll open with you
characters in the fog.

1207
01:02:07,425 --> 01:02:10,862
- Drop Mr. Toliver off.
- And don't slow down.

1208
01:02:16,867 --> 01:02:19,103
- This looks fine right here.

1209
01:02:19,137 --> 01:02:20,972
Maybe they got a typewriter
in this joint.

1210
01:02:21,005 --> 01:02:24,242
- So long characters.
- Keep the meter running.

1211
01:02:24,275 --> 01:02:25,910
- Why are we stopping here?

1212
01:02:25,943 --> 01:02:28,278
- Believe it or not,
it's what he wants.

1213
01:02:28,312 --> 01:02:31,382
- Toliver!
Come on, nobody's home!

1214
01:02:31,415 --> 01:02:33,885
- Genius waits for no one.

1215
01:02:33,918 --> 01:02:37,322
- But stupidity hammers
on deserted buildings.

1216
01:02:37,355 --> 01:02:39,423
- I'll break a window.

1217
01:02:39,457 --> 01:02:42,560
- Huh? What?

1218
01:02:42,593 --> 01:02:44,795
- Boy, I'd bet that'd be scary

1219
01:02:44,828 --> 01:02:46,330
if we could make out
what it was.

1220
01:02:47,832 --> 01:02:51,268
- Aah! Aah!

1221
01:02:51,302 --> 01:02:54,305
- Let's get out of here, Scooby.

1222
01:02:59,010 --> 01:03:01,012
[Car engine starts]

1223
01:03:06,083 --> 01:03:08,887
- I've never seen anything
like it.

1224
01:03:08,920 --> 01:03:11,255
- Even in Tijuana.
- They're not human.

1225
01:03:13,090 --> 01:03:15,560
They're monsters.

1226
01:03:15,593 --> 01:03:18,028
- People. Lots of 'em.

1227
01:03:18,062 --> 01:03:22,165
- Wish we had one of them
station wagons.

1228
01:03:22,198 --> 01:03:24,202
- Look, they're all on their way

1229
01:03:24,235 --> 01:03:26,337
to a George Romero
film festival.

1230
01:03:32,108 --> 01:03:35,212
- Get back! Get in!

1231
01:03:43,220 --> 01:03:45,557
- What the--oh, my gosh.
We're out of gas.

1232
01:03:45,590 --> 01:03:47,157
- Oh, sure. That old trick.

1233
01:03:47,190 --> 01:03:48,893
He wants to make out
with the whole family.

1234
01:03:48,927 --> 01:03:50,460
- We'll make a run for it.

1235
01:03:55,332 --> 01:03:58,335
- It's a butcher shop.
How fitting.

1236
01:04:02,940 --> 01:04:05,943
- Oh, my goodness.
Tip roast is 79 cents.

1237
01:04:05,977 --> 01:04:10,615
- Of course it's high.
It is the apocalypse, you know.

1238
01:04:12,015 --> 01:04:14,418
Or the porkalypse.

1239
01:04:17,187 --> 01:04:19,157
Chicken lips.

1240
01:04:19,190 --> 01:04:21,958
- Anyway, it's a good thing
they're in a butcher shop.

1241
01:04:21,992 --> 01:04:23,460
In case somebody
gets a black eye,

1242
01:04:23,493 --> 01:04:25,128
they'll have a steak there
they can put it on.

1243
01:04:25,162 --> 01:04:26,297
- Yeah.

1244
01:04:34,072 --> 01:04:35,907
- Seems safe enough.

1245
01:04:35,940 --> 01:04:38,242
- I'll see where this one goes,
just in case.

1246
01:04:38,275 --> 01:04:41,612
- Aah! Look!

1247
01:04:41,645 --> 01:04:44,882
- Oh, man.
You got glass in the kielbasa.

1248
01:04:46,350 --> 01:04:47,517
- Come here.

1249
01:04:47,550 --> 01:04:51,522
- Hey, what kind of price you
got on ground chuck today?

1250
01:04:51,555 --> 01:04:54,992
- They're after real low prices.

1251
01:04:55,025 --> 01:04:56,260
Has-been actor, 79 cents.

1252
01:04:56,293 --> 01:04:59,230
Juicy 18 year olds,
3.95 a pound.

1253
01:04:59,263 --> 01:05:00,998
- Just like the hero does.

1254
01:05:01,032 --> 01:05:03,467
- He's just a story.

1255
01:05:03,500 --> 01:05:06,170
Die, you monster!
You're not real!

1256
01:05:10,307 --> 01:05:12,377
Go on.

1257
01:05:12,410 --> 01:05:15,278
- Well, at least now they'll
be plenty of ham steak.

1258
01:05:15,312 --> 01:05:17,682
- Looks like Toliver
is this week's special.

1259
01:05:17,715 --> 01:05:19,550
- Tom, don't leave him out
there. You've got to help him.

1260
01:05:19,583 --> 01:05:21,452
- Stop it, Lisa! Stop it!

1261
01:05:21,485 --> 01:05:24,355
- Lisa.
- Shut up.

1262
01:05:24,388 --> 01:05:25,957
- There's nothing we can do now.

1263
01:05:25,990 --> 01:05:28,558
- Oh, we should be safe in here.
It's a meat locker.

1264
01:05:28,592 --> 01:05:31,528
Metal door back, front.
It should hold 'em.

1265
01:05:31,562 --> 01:05:33,297
- What difference does it make?

1266
01:05:33,330 --> 01:05:35,532
We're stuck in this refrigerator
with those things out there.

1267
01:05:35,565 --> 01:05:37,335
- That's enough, Bonnie.

1268
01:05:37,368 --> 01:05:39,470
Now let's get one thing
straight.

1269
01:05:39,503 --> 01:05:40,570
Toliver is dead.

1270
01:05:40,603 --> 01:05:42,440
- So?

1271
01:05:42,473 --> 01:05:44,075
- He had the same chance
for safety that we all had.

1272
01:05:44,108 --> 01:05:46,643
Is that understood?

1273
01:05:46,677 --> 01:05:48,478
Fear killed Toliver.

1274
01:05:48,512 --> 01:05:50,682
- No, I thought it was those
big guys in the costumes.

1275
01:05:50,715 --> 01:05:53,050
- I'm sorry, Tom.

1276
01:05:53,083 --> 01:05:54,652
- Me too.

1277
01:05:54,685 --> 01:05:58,088
- All right. Now if we expect
to get out of here in one piece,

1278
01:05:58,122 --> 01:05:59,757
it's going to take all the nerve
we've got and then some.

1279
01:05:59,790 --> 01:06:02,158
- You'd better order out
for more nerve.

1280
01:06:02,192 --> 01:06:04,228
- I don't expect you to act
as if nothing's happened.

1281
01:06:04,262 --> 01:06:05,697
But you've got to keep calm.

1282
01:06:09,032 --> 01:06:11,302
- Come on, Bonnie.

1283
01:06:12,703 --> 01:06:15,305
- Remember, keep cool.

1284
01:06:15,338 --> 01:06:17,375
- Oh, Fabian gets off
a good one.

1285
01:06:17,408 --> 01:06:19,577
- They're wonderful.
Both of them.

1286
01:06:19,610 --> 01:06:22,680
Tom, you'd better come with me.

1287
01:06:22,713 --> 01:06:24,748
We've got to make some plans.

1288
01:06:24,782 --> 01:06:27,283
- He asked me. He asked me.

1289
01:06:30,520 --> 01:06:33,623
- Uh, Cal, there's a bug
on your hand there.

1290
01:06:33,657 --> 01:06:35,325
Got it.

1291
01:06:37,193 --> 01:06:40,297
- Ew, what is that?
What is that stuff?

1292
01:06:40,330 --> 01:06:43,200
- It's the rest of the movie.
- ...marinating meat.

1293
01:06:43,233 --> 01:06:46,370
- Tom, did you notice
any flashing

1294
01:06:46,403 --> 01:06:49,107
or a possible electrical field
or any heat

1295
01:06:49,140 --> 01:06:50,340
when you were in the plane?

1296
01:06:50,373 --> 01:06:52,610
- No. Unless you mean my own
temperature when I almost took

1297
01:06:52,643 --> 01:06:54,812
a fast dip into the ocean.

1298
01:06:54,845 --> 01:06:57,715
- Ah, he kills me, that guy.

1299
01:06:57,748 --> 01:06:58,982
- Of course,
there is a bright side to this.

1300
01:06:59,015 --> 01:07:02,218
The first chemicals you pick off
the shelves just might do it.

1301
01:07:02,252 --> 01:07:05,422
- Professor,
I don't see any slime men,

1302
01:07:05,455 --> 01:07:07,725
- Of course I've got my eyes
squeezed tightly shut.

1303
01:07:10,627 --> 01:07:14,198
- Tom, what did you say
about your landing?

1304
01:07:14,232 --> 01:07:16,567
- The plane dove to the water.
It almost touched it.

1305
01:07:18,668 --> 01:07:22,740
- We could break their wall
with sodium chloride.

1306
01:07:22,773 --> 01:07:25,375
- Oh, you mean
ordinary table salt?

1307
01:07:25,408 --> 01:07:26,710
Ah!

1308
01:07:26,743 --> 01:07:28,578
- What's sodium chloride?

1309
01:07:28,612 --> 01:07:31,348
- Sodium chloride is table salt.

1310
01:07:31,382 --> 01:07:34,585
Salt has to be the answer.

1311
01:07:34,618 --> 01:07:36,420
'Cause the dome cannot
become solid

1312
01:07:36,453 --> 01:07:38,788
for a good number of feet
above the ocean.

1313
01:07:38,822 --> 01:07:41,458
The spray from the saltwater
won't permit it.

1314
01:07:41,492 --> 01:07:43,593
And that's how Tom got through.

1315
01:07:43,627 --> 01:07:46,497
- And why aren't the Navy subs
coming in?

1316
01:07:46,530 --> 01:07:49,333
- They probably haven't stumbled
onto it yet.

1317
01:07:49,367 --> 01:07:51,168
- Is there enough salt here,
Dad?

1318
01:07:51,202 --> 01:07:52,302
- I hope.

1319
01:07:52,335 --> 01:07:56,173
- You know, that plane
was pretty well watered down.

1320
01:07:56,207 --> 01:07:58,275
Why don't we find everything
we can to make a vehicle?

1321
01:07:58,308 --> 01:07:59,777
- But that'll take hours.

1322
01:07:59,810 --> 01:08:01,712
And we still have to find
the car.

1323
01:08:01,745 --> 01:08:04,582
We can't just walk through
slime men looking for more salt.

1324
01:08:04,615 --> 01:08:07,885
- Hey, come on. It's not the end
of the world, you Gloomy Guses.

1325
01:08:07,918 --> 01:08:10,788
- But it is.
- Doh!

1326
01:08:12,657 --> 01:08:15,758
- What are they building?
A float?

1327
01:08:15,792 --> 01:08:21,765
- Something with salt
and really get out of here.

1328
01:08:21,798 --> 01:08:25,768
- I hope she asks me
to the Sadie Hawkins dance.

1329
01:08:25,802 --> 01:08:27,370
- I'll find a car
and bring it back.

1330
01:08:27,403 --> 01:08:30,473
- I'll go with you.
- No, you stay here.

1331
01:08:30,507 --> 01:08:33,677
And don't tell them I've gone.
They'd just come looking.

1332
01:08:33,710 --> 01:08:36,380
- Why should I just sit here
and not do anything either?

1333
01:08:36,413 --> 01:08:39,450
- 'Cause you're a pawn
in a much larger game.

1334
01:08:39,483 --> 01:08:41,252
- Cal, I can help you.

1335
01:08:41,285 --> 01:08:43,887
- In ways you never
knew possible.

1336
01:08:43,920 --> 01:08:45,857
- But you stay close to me
all the time.

1337
01:08:45,890 --> 01:08:48,258
- Okay.

1338
01:08:50,260 --> 01:08:53,363
- If he starts singing
"Oh, What a Beautiful Morning"

1339
01:08:53,397 --> 01:08:54,832
I'm gonna lose it.

1340
01:09:00,837 --> 01:09:03,873
- We got to stay over this way.
Back there is the wall.

1341
01:09:03,907 --> 01:09:06,910
You stay far away from there.

1342
01:09:06,943 --> 01:09:10,915
- Kids, do not play on, in,
or around dumpsters.

1343
01:09:10,948 --> 01:09:12,515
- Behind you.

1344
01:09:12,548 --> 01:09:15,285
- Hey, white trash meets
real trash.

1345
01:09:20,958 --> 01:09:22,693
- Bonnie?

1346
01:09:22,727 --> 01:09:24,728
- Those crazy kids
walked right out.

1347
01:09:24,762 --> 01:09:25,963
- Well, they must be near.

1348
01:09:25,997 --> 01:09:27,598
It's only a minute
since they left. Unless...

1349
01:09:27,632 --> 01:09:29,833
- Oh, Tom, let's go
and find them, please.

1350
01:09:29,867 --> 01:09:31,235
- No, no. You stay here.

1351
01:09:31,268 --> 01:09:32,770
You work on the car.
Both of you.

1352
01:09:32,803 --> 01:09:34,605
Makes more sense that way.
Honey, hand me that gun.

1353
01:09:34,638 --> 01:09:36,940
- Stay up at the counter
in case we have some customers.

1354
01:09:36,973 --> 01:09:38,875
- You be careful, Tom.
- I will.

1355
01:09:38,908 --> 01:09:41,812
- Um...dumpster.

1356
01:09:41,845 --> 01:09:43,380
- Dumpster, ah.

1357
01:09:43,413 --> 01:09:45,615
- Look out!
- Dumpster. [Mumbling]

1358
01:09:47,852 --> 01:09:50,820
- Bonnie!

1359
01:09:50,853 --> 01:09:52,757
Bonnie!

1360
01:09:52,790 --> 01:09:54,425
- Dumpster!
- Watch out!

1361
01:10:01,432 --> 01:10:03,367
- Professor!

1362
01:10:03,400 --> 01:10:04,702
- How do slime monsters
get any traction?

1363
01:10:04,735 --> 01:10:07,237
- Hey, no deliveries
between 10:00 and 2:00.

1364
01:10:11,275 --> 01:10:14,312
Bonnie? Cal, where's Bonnie?

1365
01:10:14,345 --> 01:10:17,313
I don't know.
She was right there with me.

1366
01:10:17,347 --> 01:10:18,715
And then she was gone.

1367
01:10:18,748 --> 01:10:20,985
- I ran away
like a yellow belly.

1368
01:10:21,018 --> 01:10:22,585
- You didn't see any sign of--

1369
01:10:22,618 --> 01:10:25,855
Cal, she's not dead.

1370
01:10:25,888 --> 01:10:28,325
- She's probably having
the slime of her life.

1371
01:10:28,358 --> 01:10:31,862
- Oh, no. Not Bonnie.
- Well, parts of Bonnie.

1372
01:10:31,895 --> 01:10:34,632
- They've taken her.
Probably to try and lure us

1373
01:10:34,665 --> 01:10:35,967
out in the open.

1374
01:10:36,000 --> 01:10:37,902
As long as they keep her alive,

1375
01:10:37,935 --> 01:10:39,470
they know we'll come out
after her.

1376
01:10:39,503 --> 01:10:41,805
- Where do you think
you're going?

1377
01:10:41,838 --> 01:10:44,273
- I'm going after her.
I got her into this.

1378
01:10:44,307 --> 01:10:46,543
Now I'll get her out.
See this?

1379
01:10:46,577 --> 01:10:49,447
They used it on us.
I'll use it right back on them.

1380
01:10:49,480 --> 01:10:51,848
- Get back here.

1381
01:10:54,552 --> 01:10:57,587
- Would you like a copy
of our Watchtower?

1382
01:10:57,620 --> 01:11:01,425
- Lobster claws on sale.
2.19 a pound.

1383
01:11:01,458 --> 01:11:02,893
- Don't touch it, Tom.

1384
01:11:02,927 --> 01:11:04,795
- A good salesmen always
sticks his foot in the door.

1385
01:11:04,828 --> 01:11:07,430
- They're self-sealing.
- What? You mean?

1386
01:11:07,463 --> 01:11:09,867
A bullet makes a hole and then
closes up, is that right?

1387
01:11:09,900 --> 01:11:11,402
- That has got to hurt.

1388
01:11:11,435 --> 01:11:13,003
- Those are good
with drawn butter.

1389
01:11:13,037 --> 01:11:16,040
- When they fight among
themselves, the cap is removed,

1390
01:11:16,073 --> 01:11:19,042
the lance is hollow. Therefore
the hole doesn't close.

1391
01:11:19,075 --> 01:11:20,843
- Okay, now we know.

1392
01:11:20,877 --> 01:11:24,482
- He knows everything.
- ...I'm gonna go after Bonnie.

1393
01:11:24,515 --> 01:11:26,317
- Well, you're not going to help
her by getting yourself killed.

1394
01:11:26,350 --> 01:11:27,317
Those creatures are right
outside that door.

1395
01:11:27,350 --> 01:11:28,785
They'll get you in a minute.

1396
01:11:28,818 --> 01:11:30,387
- Well, we just can't
let them have her!

1397
01:11:30,420 --> 01:11:32,622
- I'll bet they've got
a headquarters nearby.

1398
01:11:39,663 --> 01:11:42,398
- Oh, my god.
Corned beef. 89 cents.

1399
01:11:42,432 --> 01:11:44,435
Joel, I can't believe this.

1400
01:12:01,118 --> 01:12:04,555
- Another victim of slime!

1401
01:12:07,090 --> 01:12:08,858
- Let's get him out of here.

1402
01:12:12,595 --> 01:12:16,767
- Nice guys. Thanks.

1403
01:12:20,870 --> 01:12:25,542
- How do you fight lightning
speed slime people like that?

1404
01:12:29,580 --> 01:12:30,813
- Better around that pole.

1405
01:12:30,847 --> 01:12:32,715
- Hubcap.

1406
01:12:32,748 --> 01:12:35,452
- They'll come back for it.

1407
01:12:35,485 --> 01:12:38,088
- He got the tire.

1408
01:12:38,122 --> 01:12:40,957
- You will believe
a Marine can drive.

1409
01:12:40,990 --> 01:12:43,693
- Daddy! Daddy!

1410
01:12:43,727 --> 01:12:45,928
- Shut up, I'm your daddy now.

1411
01:12:45,962 --> 01:12:50,000
- Daddy! Daddy!

1412
01:12:50,033 --> 01:12:51,868
Daddy!

1413
01:12:59,810 --> 01:13:02,112
- The car's not moving.

1414
01:13:02,145 --> 01:13:06,183
- Don't stop the car.
Let 'em think we're still in it.

1415
01:13:06,217 --> 01:13:08,452
- Bad shocks.

1416
01:13:15,092 --> 01:13:18,528
- Oh, they saw Commando Cody.

1417
01:13:18,562 --> 01:13:20,863
- They gave the slime the slip.

1418
01:13:26,203 --> 01:13:27,872
- Cal, come here.

1419
01:13:31,942 --> 01:13:34,143
- Feel all right?
- I'm all right.

1420
01:13:34,177 --> 01:13:36,780
- Now look,
we've got to find their trail.

1421
01:13:36,813 --> 01:13:39,015
Footprints, slime, anything.

1422
01:13:39,048 --> 01:13:42,618
- Discarded Kleenex,
antihistamines, Neo-Synephrine.

1423
01:13:42,652 --> 01:13:44,555
Keep looking.

1424
01:13:50,527 --> 01:13:51,928
- What are we seeing?

1425
01:13:53,563 --> 01:13:56,833
- The future.
- Oh.

1426
01:14:03,707 --> 01:14:06,210
- Aah!
- Come on, baby.

1427
01:14:06,243 --> 01:14:08,212
We could have
a nice life together.

1428
01:14:08,245 --> 01:14:10,147
These scales aren't so bad
once you get used to them.

1429
01:14:10,180 --> 01:14:13,950
You'll callus up in a while.
- Aah!

1430
01:14:13,983 --> 01:14:16,085
Aah!

1431
01:14:16,118 --> 01:14:20,223
- Honestly, Bonnie,
the slime you bring home.

1432
01:14:26,630 --> 01:14:30,600
- A slime field outside of L.A.
and Milwaukee, Wisconsin,

1433
01:14:30,633 --> 01:14:32,802
are a thousand miles apart.

1434
01:14:32,835 --> 01:14:35,238
But they both mean one thing
to these guys.

1435
01:14:35,272 --> 01:14:39,008
And that's cold Milwaukee beer.

1436
01:14:41,143 --> 01:14:43,180
- Slime light.

1437
01:14:47,183 --> 01:14:49,920
- Slimeiken.

1438
01:14:51,120 --> 01:14:53,557
- Look at this. Blond hair.

1439
01:14:53,590 --> 01:14:56,893
- Yeah, you can usually find a
blond hair in a field of wheat.

1440
01:14:56,927 --> 01:14:59,930
- At night.
- In a fog.

1441
01:14:59,963 --> 01:15:02,498
- Come on. Let's go.

1442
01:15:04,902 --> 01:15:08,672
[Screaming]

1443
01:15:13,177 --> 01:15:15,878
- What was that?
[Screaming continues]

1444
01:15:15,912 --> 01:15:19,315
- I'll give you something
to scream about.

1445
01:15:19,348 --> 01:15:21,785
- He'll never make
Slime's Man of the Year.

1446
01:15:53,150 --> 01:15:55,052
- Oh, it's a good thing
they don't have eyes

1447
01:15:55,085 --> 01:15:58,855
on the back of their
exoskeletons.

1448
01:15:58,888 --> 01:16:02,192
- No professor's daughter
is worth that.

1449
01:16:02,225 --> 01:16:04,327
Let's make tracks, guys.

1450
01:16:05,962 --> 01:16:07,863
- Speaking of which,
we'd better go pretty soon.

1451
01:16:07,897 --> 01:16:11,668
- Let's get out of here.

1452
01:16:11,702 --> 01:16:13,103
- Let's get out of here.

1453
01:16:13,137 --> 01:16:15,238
- We'd better get out of here.

1454
01:16:15,272 --> 01:16:18,842
- Bye, guys.
Meet you back at the lodge.

1455
01:16:21,010 --> 01:16:24,682
[Screaming continues]

1456
01:16:44,802 --> 01:16:46,803
- What's with all this smoke?
Get up here, you guys.

1457
01:16:46,837 --> 01:16:49,005
Did you put a crack
in the bipolar defroculator

1458
01:16:49,038 --> 01:16:50,273
or something?

1459
01:16:50,307 --> 01:16:53,243
- Joel? What's the matter, Joel?
You scared?

1460
01:16:53,277 --> 01:16:54,310
- No, I'm not scared.

1461
01:16:54,343 --> 01:16:57,113
I just wonder what
all this smoke's about. Come on.

1462
01:16:57,147 --> 01:16:59,148
- I'd be scared if I were you,
Joel.

1463
01:16:59,182 --> 01:17:02,352
The slime people could be
somewhere in the fog.

1464
01:17:02,385 --> 01:17:06,088
- Yeah, Joel, nothing scarier
than a big, boiled lobster

1465
01:17:06,122 --> 01:17:08,858
with a bad, oozing head cold,
a big, scaly wart,

1466
01:17:08,892 --> 01:17:10,860
and crusted crab monster
leaking mucous--

1467
01:17:10,893 --> 01:17:12,862
- Okay.
Listen, okay, I get the point.

1468
01:17:12,895 --> 01:17:14,698
Now, come on. I'm not scared.

1469
01:17:14,732 --> 01:17:16,867
But I just got to know
what's going on.

1470
01:17:16,900 --> 01:17:18,335
Well, the truth is, Joel,

1471
01:17:18,368 --> 01:17:20,937
we rigged Gypsy's thermal
conducer tube to create big,

1472
01:17:20,970 --> 01:17:23,407
billowy clouds
of terrifying slime smoke.

1473
01:17:23,440 --> 01:17:24,640
Pretty funny, huh?

1474
01:17:24,673 --> 01:17:27,277
- Yeah. Well, you guys have
to learn there's nothing scary

1475
01:17:27,310 --> 01:17:29,112
about a bunch of
out-of-work actors

1476
01:17:29,145 --> 01:17:32,148
parading around with
papier-mâché skin conditions.

1477
01:17:32,182 --> 01:17:34,250
- Yeah, they do kind of look
like Quasimodo with eczema,

1478
01:17:34,283 --> 01:17:35,752
don't they?

1479
01:17:35,785 --> 01:17:37,453
- Yeah, like a tree toad
with psoriasis.

1480
01:17:37,487 --> 01:17:40,690
- Exactly. Subterranean
alligators in body suits

1481
01:17:40,723 --> 01:17:42,325
come from underground
to make better lives

1482
01:17:42,358 --> 01:17:44,160
for themselves in L.A.

1483
01:17:44,193 --> 01:17:46,128
Unfortunately, the only way
they know how to communicate

1484
01:17:46,162 --> 01:17:48,265
is by impaling people
with prehistoric spears.

1485
01:17:48,298 --> 01:17:50,300
So they're not really so bad.
They're just misunderstood,

1486
01:17:50,333 --> 01:17:51,500
I think.

1487
01:17:51,533 --> 01:17:55,072
- Say, Joel, how come they have
a machine that can create

1488
01:17:55,105 --> 01:17:57,440
a solid rock-like wall
out of fog,

1489
01:17:57,473 --> 01:18:00,077
but they can't create,
oh say, a gun?

1490
01:18:00,110 --> 01:18:03,213
- Well, Crow, that's simple.
It's easy to understand.

1491
01:18:03,247 --> 01:18:05,048
'Cause it's just a movie.

1492
01:18:05,082 --> 01:18:06,282
- Oh, yeah.

1493
01:18:06,315 --> 01:18:08,818
- All right, I got to go check
on Gypsy so this whole ship

1494
01:18:08,852 --> 01:18:10,287
doesn't fill with smoke.

1495
01:18:10,320 --> 01:18:11,455
- You know, Crow,
maybe next time

1496
01:18:11,488 --> 01:18:12,888
we should fill up the bridge

1497
01:18:12,922 --> 01:18:15,325
with deli style lemon whip
to scare 'em.

1498
01:18:15,358 --> 01:18:18,027
- Yeah! Or 7-layer salad.
- Yeah, that's a good idea.

1499
01:18:18,060 --> 01:18:19,328
- Or taco.
- With peas and stuff.

1500
01:18:19,362 --> 01:18:23,167
- We got commercial sign.
- Whoa.

1501
01:18:36,312 --> 01:18:38,815
- Are you all right?
- Sure, honey.

1502
01:18:38,848 --> 01:18:41,852
- They found Bonnie.

1503
01:18:41,885 --> 01:18:45,155
- Here you go.
- Believe me, it works.

1504
01:18:45,188 --> 01:18:47,957
- It's a good thing she's
the only human they didn't kill.

1505
01:18:47,990 --> 01:18:49,358
- Come on.

1506
01:18:57,000 --> 01:19:00,837
- Bonnie, I'm sorry about
the whole dumpster slime thing.

1507
01:19:07,210 --> 01:19:08,878
- Look at that!

1508
01:19:08,912 --> 01:19:10,847
- I told you we should've
of put the campfire out.

1509
01:19:10,880 --> 01:19:11,948
- The machine
that makes the wall.

1510
01:19:11,982 --> 01:19:14,150
- It's the Play-Doh Fun Factory.

1511
01:19:21,457 --> 01:19:24,260
- Tip O'Neil 79 cents a pound.

1512
01:19:24,293 --> 01:19:26,395
- Francis Bacon,
three for a dollar.

1513
01:19:26,428 --> 01:19:28,365
- Lancelot Links, 2.49 a pound.

1514
01:19:32,835 --> 01:19:33,903
- Open up.

1515
01:19:37,007 --> 01:19:39,542
- Oh, Bonnie,
are you all right?

1516
01:19:39,575 --> 01:19:41,377
- Yeah, just a little slimy.

1517
01:19:41,410 --> 01:19:45,082
- Now, don't be afraid dear.
You're safe now.

1518
01:19:45,115 --> 01:19:46,215
Don't cry, dear.
You're all right.

1519
01:19:46,248 --> 01:19:48,585
- They kept dragging me around,
the sat me in the cave.

1520
01:19:48,618 --> 01:19:50,387
They were all around.
I couldn't get out!

1521
01:19:50,420 --> 01:19:53,357
- Don't, don't. We'll get
out of everything all right.

1522
01:19:53,390 --> 01:19:55,958
- I feel all oogy, Daddy.

1523
01:19:55,992 --> 01:19:57,960
- Lisa,
I think she'd feel better

1524
01:19:57,993 --> 01:19:59,428
if you'd help her clean up
a little bit.

1525
01:19:59,462 --> 01:20:02,332
- I think we'd all feel better
if we cleaned her up a bit.

1526
01:20:04,333 --> 01:20:06,468
- If we'd only evacuated
with the others.

1527
01:20:06,502 --> 01:20:08,170
None of this would've happened.

1528
01:20:08,203 --> 01:20:10,340
- Well, I haven't figured out
anything really.

1529
01:20:10,373 --> 01:20:12,442
Except I marinated
a few t-bones.

1530
01:20:12,475 --> 01:20:14,510
- From now on I want you
to follow orders, okay.

1531
01:20:14,543 --> 01:20:17,013
- Wait, I'm the Marine.
You're the sports reporter.

1532
01:20:17,047 --> 01:20:18,415
- That makes sense.

1533
01:20:18,448 --> 01:20:20,317
- I think we found
their machine.

1534
01:20:20,350 --> 01:20:22,052
- The machine that makes
the wall.

1535
01:20:22,085 --> 01:20:23,453
- What did it look like?

1536
01:20:23,487 --> 01:20:25,188
Were there any dials,
any levers,

1537
01:20:25,222 --> 01:20:27,323
anything we could reverse
to break the wall?

1538
01:20:27,357 --> 01:20:28,858
- The slime men were
coming back.

1539
01:20:28,892 --> 01:20:30,193
And we had to leave.

1540
01:20:30,227 --> 01:20:32,428
- Turned and ran again, huh?

1541
01:20:32,462 --> 01:20:34,330
- ...large, soft mass that came
out of some kind of a machine.

1542
01:20:34,363 --> 01:20:36,298
I've never seen anything
like it before.

1543
01:20:36,332 --> 01:20:38,200
- That means we'll have to rely
on the salt.

1544
01:20:38,233 --> 01:20:40,470
- Let's try it.
- Wait a minute, Cal.

1545
01:20:40,503 --> 01:20:43,940
- How much salt do we have?
- Two buckets of solution.

1546
01:20:43,973 --> 01:20:46,108
- And a lot of those little,
tiny take-out packets.

1547
01:20:46,142 --> 01:20:48,043
- No, we haven't.

1548
01:20:48,077 --> 01:20:50,080
The slime people already have
our temperature under control.

1549
01:20:50,113 --> 01:20:53,383
- And they're not very gentle
when they use a thermometer.

1550
01:20:53,417 --> 01:20:55,485
- As long as we stay in here,
we're just setting ducks.

1551
01:20:55,518 --> 01:20:57,620
- No, sitting ducks.

1552
01:20:57,653 --> 01:21:00,090
- Where is this salt?
- Outside the door.

1553
01:21:00,123 --> 01:21:02,992
- I'll round them up.
- Come on, Lisa, Bonnie.

1554
01:21:03,025 --> 01:21:04,393
We're going through
that wall right now.

1555
01:21:04,427 --> 01:21:09,065
- Aren't men cool?
- Gals, bring those buckets.

1556
01:21:09,098 --> 01:21:11,100
- From now on
we call it sodium chloride.

1557
01:21:11,133 --> 01:21:13,437
It's sort of a code. They won't
know what we're talking about.

1558
01:21:30,187 --> 01:21:32,688
- Keep the buckets and your
purses separate everybody.

1559
01:21:32,722 --> 01:21:36,358
We don't won't to douse
the slime people with cosmetics.

1560
01:21:36,392 --> 01:21:38,127
- What happened to their car?

1561
01:21:38,160 --> 01:21:41,965
- Wrong way Marine guy decided
to let it drop off a cliff.

1562
01:21:55,545 --> 01:21:58,582
- That was a gripping scene.

1563
01:21:58,615 --> 01:22:01,250
- Well, here it is again
from a different angle.

1564
01:22:04,453 --> 01:22:06,957
- Why are those guys
still carrying guns?

1565
01:22:06,990 --> 01:22:09,725
They don't have any affect on
the slime people, we know that.

1566
01:22:09,758 --> 01:22:11,962
- It's so they don't have
to carry the buckets.

1567
01:22:11,995 --> 01:22:13,663
- Oh.

1568
01:22:13,697 --> 01:22:17,200
- Crafty.
- To a fault.

1569
01:22:20,070 --> 01:22:22,038
- Uh, what's
the professor carrying?

1570
01:22:22,072 --> 01:22:24,573
- Ah, the movie?
- A grudge maybe.

1571
01:22:24,607 --> 01:22:26,175
- The weight of the world.

1572
01:22:47,697 --> 01:22:49,665
- Oh a crime in etly, Joel.

1573
01:22:49,698 --> 01:22:51,735
What is this,
the Bataan death march?

1574
01:22:51,768 --> 01:22:54,537
- I think it's
the Bataan death watch.

1575
01:23:03,345 --> 01:23:05,248
- Quiet now.

1576
01:23:05,282 --> 01:23:07,217
- The headquarters
are right below us.

1577
01:23:07,250 --> 01:23:09,452
- Think they're using
a fog filter here?

1578
01:23:09,485 --> 01:23:13,288
- Girls, bring
the salt solution over here.

1579
01:23:13,322 --> 01:23:15,358
- Dad, what is all that noise.

1580
01:23:15,392 --> 01:23:18,127
- The military trying to break
through from the outside.

1581
01:23:18,160 --> 01:23:19,462
- Cal, did you bring
the rags and sticks?

1582
01:23:19,495 --> 01:23:20,630
- I got 'em.

1583
01:23:20,663 --> 01:23:22,332
- All right,
make some of those sticks.

1584
01:23:22,365 --> 01:23:24,400
Keep them well-soaked
in this solution

1585
01:23:24,433 --> 01:23:26,235
and apply them to this area
right here.

1586
01:23:26,268 --> 01:23:27,637
- Your thighs?

1587
01:23:27,670 --> 01:23:29,338
- Professor, come here.

1588
01:23:32,408 --> 01:23:34,477
- What is it, Tom?

1589
01:23:34,510 --> 01:23:37,180
- I'm going down there
and get their machine.

1590
01:23:37,213 --> 01:23:39,482
- It's too much of a risk.
- Not right now.

1591
01:23:39,515 --> 01:23:41,683
The cave was clear
when we came up here.

1592
01:23:41,717 --> 01:23:45,322
They're probably still trying to
figure out where we took Bonnie.

1593
01:23:45,355 --> 01:23:47,723
- We'll try the salt first.
If that doesn't work--

1594
01:23:47,757 --> 01:23:48,790
- No, no,
there's not enough time.

1595
01:23:48,823 --> 01:23:52,162
- The ground pepper.
- The machine is down there.

1596
01:23:52,195 --> 01:23:53,830
- You know the odds of breaking
through that wall

1597
01:23:53,863 --> 01:23:55,665
with two buckets of solution?

1598
01:23:55,698 --> 01:23:59,268
- Dad, we've almost used
half a pale.

1599
01:23:59,302 --> 01:24:01,403
You've only made a small dent.

1600
01:24:01,437 --> 01:24:04,140
- Yeah,
but the odds have dropped.

1601
01:24:04,173 --> 01:24:05,675
- I'm going after the machine.

1602
01:24:05,708 --> 01:24:08,377
- Tom, you can't.
- Honey, I have to.

1603
01:24:08,410 --> 01:24:10,078
- The end of the movie
depends on it.

1604
01:24:10,112 --> 01:24:12,482
- Cover me from the ridge.
- Cover you? I'm going with you.

1605
01:24:12,515 --> 01:24:14,117
- No, you're not.
And that's an order.

1606
01:24:14,150 --> 01:24:15,752
You'll stay here and fire
if you run into any trouble.

1607
01:24:15,785 --> 01:24:17,587
- Wouldn't it be weird
if they just cleaned off

1608
01:24:17,620 --> 01:24:20,690
the camera lens
and all that fog went away?

1609
01:24:20,723 --> 01:24:23,258
- There are a lot of things
I want to tell you.

1610
01:24:23,292 --> 01:24:26,428
- And they all rhyme
with the letter k.

1611
01:24:26,462 --> 01:24:30,098
- All right.

1612
01:24:30,132 --> 01:24:32,368
- Kissing you is like plunging
my tongue

1613
01:24:32,402 --> 01:24:34,837
into a vat
of Shiitake mushrooms.

1614
01:24:34,870 --> 01:24:37,473
- Ick.

1615
01:24:37,507 --> 01:24:39,808
- Come on, Marine.

1616
01:24:39,842 --> 01:24:43,278
- Don't call me Marine. Geez.

1617
01:24:45,482 --> 01:24:47,783
- This looks like one long,
boring commercial

1618
01:24:47,817 --> 01:24:49,618
for London Fog raincoats.

1619
01:24:49,652 --> 01:24:52,822
- No sign of them yet. You know,
you'd better let me go.

1620
01:24:52,855 --> 01:24:54,423
You may run into trouble.

1621
01:24:54,457 --> 01:24:55,792
- Oh, you really think I might?

1622
01:24:55,825 --> 01:24:58,327
Like these last two hours
didn't count as trouble?

1623
01:24:58,360 --> 01:25:00,363
- Now look, don't leave
no matter what happens.

1624
01:25:00,397 --> 01:25:01,865
Give me a chance to get
down there

1625
01:25:01,898 --> 01:25:03,700
and then signal you
if the coast is clear.

1626
01:25:03,733 --> 01:25:05,635
- You understand?
- Yes. But--

1627
01:25:05,668 --> 01:25:07,337
- No arguments.

1628
01:25:07,370 --> 01:25:10,873
- So let's see.
I tug on the rope twice.

1629
01:25:10,907 --> 01:25:14,208
- Oh, that was hours ago,
you idiot.

1630
01:25:18,280 --> 01:25:21,217
- Cal, do you think
he'll be all right?

1631
01:25:21,250 --> 01:25:22,918
- He can take care of himself.

1632
01:25:22,952 --> 01:25:24,687
- But he's all alone.

1633
01:25:24,720 --> 01:25:26,722
- We'd better get back
to the wall, dear.

1634
01:25:26,755 --> 01:25:29,792
- I think one of us should've
gone down there to help him.

1635
01:25:31,527 --> 01:25:33,897
- Aah!
- Get back!

1636
01:25:33,930 --> 01:25:35,598
I'll take care of them.

1637
01:25:35,632 --> 01:25:39,435
- Well, it looks like Mr. Slime
Monster really came to play.

1638
01:25:39,468 --> 01:25:41,905
Remember, folks, he's battling
with a bad head cold.

1639
01:25:41,938 --> 01:25:43,807
- Oh, look at that.
A blistering backhand.

1640
01:25:43,840 --> 01:25:47,310
- Luckily the Marine has
the fan support in this case.

1641
01:25:49,412 --> 01:25:51,313
- Up-ended him.

1642
01:25:54,483 --> 01:25:58,253
- I think it's going
to be close here.

1643
01:25:58,287 --> 01:26:01,257
Yes, it's a pole vault
to the face.

1644
01:26:01,290 --> 01:26:03,292
- And a mucous rollover.

1645
01:26:11,900 --> 01:26:15,372
- Ooh, a phlegm twist.

1646
01:26:15,405 --> 01:26:17,440
- Oh, look at this.

1647
01:26:17,473 --> 01:26:21,743
Cal is clearly unfamiliar
with the subterranean rules.

1648
01:26:21,777 --> 01:26:25,282
Proves my point, though.
Slime people have no traction.

1649
01:26:27,983 --> 01:26:31,420
- Cal, that noise will probably
bring more of them.

1650
01:26:31,453 --> 01:26:33,288
- They're down near
the bowels of the commercial.

1651
01:26:33,322 --> 01:26:34,957
- Yes, sir. It's not far.
- Let's go.

1652
01:26:34,990 --> 01:26:36,625
We can't stay here any longer.

1653
01:26:36,658 --> 01:26:38,327
- Follow me.

1654
01:26:49,738 --> 01:26:51,407
- Well,
if we meet more slime people,

1655
01:26:51,440 --> 01:26:53,743
we'll just roll 'em down
the hill. They hate that.

1656
01:26:59,815 --> 01:27:02,252
- Look, the Three Spooges!

1657
01:27:02,285 --> 01:27:04,853
- All right, porcupine,
you go that way.

1658
01:27:04,887 --> 01:27:07,623
- Spinachhead, you head
over to the right there.

1659
01:27:29,078 --> 01:27:32,715
- All right, batter up.

1660
01:27:35,485 --> 01:27:38,253
Oh, I saw this
on Star Trek once.

1661
01:27:46,095 --> 01:27:50,800
[Imitates monster mumbling]

1662
01:27:51,933 --> 01:27:54,937
- Is that your stomach?
- No.

1663
01:27:58,373 --> 01:28:03,345
- No, I don't want to sing
"The Happy Wanderer" song again.

1664
01:28:08,483 --> 01:28:10,687
- Aah!

1665
01:28:14,123 --> 01:28:16,792
- Stand here. Take this.

1666
01:28:16,825 --> 01:28:19,362
- I'm going to go down
and screw things up.

1667
01:28:28,437 --> 01:28:31,840
- Kind of like fighting
a big, old booger.

1668
01:28:31,873 --> 01:28:35,010
- Hey, that was gross, Crow.

1669
01:28:35,043 --> 01:28:39,648
- Oh, a vicious, slimy right!

1670
01:28:50,727 --> 01:28:53,830
- Oh, man.
He doesn't fight fair at all.

1671
01:28:53,863 --> 01:28:56,798
I think he just snagged on him.

1672
01:29:00,035 --> 01:29:03,638
- All right, fishboy,
this is for you.

1673
01:29:11,913 --> 01:29:14,850
- Aah! Aah!

1674
01:29:18,587 --> 01:29:21,357
- Nobody messes with our Swedes.

1675
01:29:28,763 --> 01:29:30,767
- Hey, that's cashmere.

1676
01:29:33,168 --> 01:29:35,672
- Stop it.

1677
01:29:35,705 --> 01:29:37,940
Stop it!

1678
01:29:41,943 --> 01:29:44,513
- Nice tag there.

1679
01:29:46,048 --> 01:29:49,552
- Better one, better two.

1680
01:29:51,087 --> 01:29:53,890
- Looks more like one
of the first costumes

1681
01:29:53,923 --> 01:29:56,592
that didn't work out right.

1682
01:30:10,540 --> 01:30:11,973
- It's angry.

1683
01:30:12,007 --> 01:30:14,710
- They're trying freeze us
into another wall.

1684
01:30:14,743 --> 01:30:16,745
- It's the Batmos.

1685
01:30:16,778 --> 01:30:19,782
- Whoa,
like popping a zit, boys!

1686
01:30:22,618 --> 01:30:24,820
- Oh, no. Does this mean
we have to see clearly

1687
01:30:24,853 --> 01:30:26,722
for the rest of the film?

1688
01:30:26,755 --> 01:30:28,825
- What?

1689
01:30:28,858 --> 01:30:31,527
The fog is lifting.

1690
01:30:31,560 --> 01:30:33,528
- We broke the wall.

1691
01:30:33,562 --> 01:30:36,465
- It's turning back into vapor.

1692
01:30:36,498 --> 01:30:38,700
You know,
the machine did control it.

1693
01:30:47,243 --> 01:30:50,112
- Come on, you big,
lovable--huh?

1694
01:30:57,052 --> 01:30:59,722
- Ew, I kissed the Marine.

1695
01:30:59,755 --> 01:31:01,123
- Now that the fog has lifted,

1696
01:31:01,157 --> 01:31:03,960
those slime people are about
as scary as Teddy Ruxpin.

1697
01:31:15,070 --> 01:31:18,107
- Oh, but that a man might--ugh!

1698
01:31:21,877 --> 01:31:25,480
- And now it's Bonnie time.

1699
01:31:29,285 --> 01:31:32,588
Time to head for the best girl
you can find.

1700
01:31:36,225 --> 01:31:37,627
- Are you all right?

1701
01:31:37,660 --> 01:31:40,863
- Sure. But look at them.

1702
01:31:40,897 --> 01:31:46,702
- A horse! A horse!
My kingdom for a--aah!

1703
01:31:47,903 --> 01:31:49,638
- Planes.

1704
01:31:52,308 --> 01:31:54,110
- It's the jets.
- The wall is gone.

1705
01:31:55,778 --> 01:31:59,015
- We can get through now.
Let's go.

1706
01:32:03,852 --> 01:32:07,590
- Oh, and that's
the tanks we get. Heh heh.

1707
01:32:13,997 --> 01:32:16,265
- Oh, Bonnie, I got to take off.

1708
01:32:16,298 --> 01:32:18,600
These guys might be
looking for me.

1709
01:32:18,633 --> 01:32:20,168
A little matter of leaving

1710
01:32:20,202 --> 01:32:21,270
the platoon without orders,
you know.

1711
01:32:21,303 --> 01:32:24,640
- I'm glad that's over with.

1712
01:32:24,673 --> 01:32:26,742
- You know what I'm going to do
when we get home?

1713
01:32:26,775 --> 01:32:28,177
- Huh?
- What's that?

1714
01:32:28,210 --> 01:32:30,178
- Buy gallons and gallons
of salt

1715
01:32:30,212 --> 01:32:31,780
and save it for a foggy day.

1716
01:32:31,813 --> 01:32:33,915
- Funny!

1717
01:32:33,948 --> 01:32:37,052
- Let's hope you never have
to use it again, Bonnie.

1718
01:32:37,085 --> 01:32:38,920
- Dad, you don't suppose
they could come back?

1719
01:32:38,953 --> 01:32:41,723
- I hardly think so.
But who knows?

1720
01:32:41,757 --> 01:32:44,093
Perhaps there are other even
stranger creatures underground.

1721
01:32:44,127 --> 01:32:46,328
- Thank you, Mr. Optimist.

1722
01:32:46,362 --> 01:32:48,730
- Dad, you're
being a scientist again.

1723
01:32:48,763 --> 01:32:51,333
- Looks like the situation
is well in hand.

1724
01:32:51,367 --> 01:32:54,203
- They're dropping turkeys.

1725
01:32:54,237 --> 01:32:57,340
- Well, Cal,
you've been really worthless,

1726
01:32:57,373 --> 01:33:00,743
a coward, and a tower of Jell-O.
Thanks for your help.

1727
01:33:05,748 --> 01:33:07,783
- Did you actually break
this wall?

1728
01:33:07,817 --> 01:33:09,918
- No, Professor Galbraith did,
Colonel.

1729
01:33:09,952 --> 01:33:11,953
- But I was hiding behind him
when he did it.

1730
01:33:11,987 --> 01:33:13,622
- The general is waiting
to see you.

1731
01:33:13,655 --> 01:33:15,190
We'll need a full report.
Will you please come with me?

1732
01:33:15,223 --> 01:33:16,223
- With pleasure, sir.

1733
01:33:16,257 --> 01:33:20,095
- Well, there goes the last
of the wall, Tom.

1734
01:33:20,128 --> 01:33:22,263
- This is just the beginning
for us.

1735
01:33:24,133 --> 01:33:25,233
- Brother.

1736
01:33:25,267 --> 01:33:27,703
- Come on,
let's get out of here.

1737
01:33:30,405 --> 01:33:33,075
The love jeep.

1738
01:33:36,445 --> 01:33:37,980
- Oh, we better get out of here.

1739
01:33:38,013 --> 01:33:40,282
- Hey, Cal, there's a bottle of
Jack under the seat.

1740
01:34:11,313 --> 01:34:14,417
- You know, Crow, I can always
remember back on earth

1741
01:34:14,450 --> 01:34:16,685
the way the house was filled
with the smells

1742
01:34:16,718 --> 01:34:18,253
of Mom's home cooking.

1743
01:34:18,287 --> 01:34:20,890
- You mean your mom would cook
your home?

1744
01:34:20,923 --> 01:34:22,958
- Uh, no, Crow,
it's just an expression.

1745
01:34:22,992 --> 01:34:26,695
It's like nothin' means lovin'
like somethin' from the oven.

1746
01:34:26,728 --> 01:34:29,798
- Ah, yeah, an old expression.
I know one of those.

1747
01:34:29,832 --> 01:34:32,000
There as an old man
from Nantucket--

1748
01:34:32,033 --> 01:34:34,170
- No, bonk. Sorry.

1749
01:34:34,203 --> 01:34:35,772
No, it's nothing like that.

1750
01:34:35,805 --> 01:34:37,338
Anyway,
I thought it'd be fun today

1751
01:34:37,372 --> 01:34:41,043
to make a silicon diode pie
with a light rectifier crust.

1752
01:34:41,077 --> 01:34:43,012
- Oh, you're going to put
resistors in it again?

1753
01:34:43,045 --> 01:34:45,113
- Yeah. Got some right here.
You don't like that--

1754
01:34:45,147 --> 01:34:49,285
Anyway, take the resistors,
roll 'em out with a rolling pin.

1755
01:34:49,318 --> 01:34:51,320
- Ooh, that makes
the crust crunchy.

1756
01:34:51,353 --> 01:34:54,090
- Yeah. Put them in
a special silicon pan.

1757
01:34:54,123 --> 01:34:59,095
Heat up your crystal chamber
for 20,000 degrees.

1758
01:34:59,128 --> 01:35:01,197
And we'll put that in.
And I made one earlier.

1759
01:35:01,230 --> 01:35:03,865
I want to show you.
I think I have it right here.

1760
01:35:03,898 --> 01:35:06,468
It's right here cooling
on the window shade.

1761
01:35:06,502 --> 01:35:07,903
Window sill.

1762
01:35:07,937 --> 01:35:09,372
- Oh.
- Oh, man.

1763
01:35:09,405 --> 01:35:11,940
Gypsy
she ate almost half of it.

1764
01:35:11,973 --> 01:35:14,343
- Yeah, and the other half's
on her mouth.

1765
01:35:14,377 --> 01:35:16,878
- I'm sorry, Joel.
- You're such a messy eater.

1766
01:35:16,912 --> 01:35:20,215
Anyway, that's okay.
We got a letter to read here.

1767
01:35:20,248 --> 01:35:21,950
- Read the one from
Deus Ex Machina.

1768
01:35:21,983 --> 01:35:24,018
- Right, good one.
No, this wasn't from David.

1769
01:35:24,052 --> 01:35:27,857
This is from Sarah McCall
from North Carolina.

1770
01:35:27,890 --> 01:35:31,827
She writes,
"Dear MST 3000, Hi, how are you?

1771
01:35:31,860 --> 01:35:34,430
"My name is Sarah,
and I'm 14 years old.

1772
01:35:34,463 --> 01:35:38,400
"I love MST 3000. It's so good.

1773
01:35:38,433 --> 01:35:41,037
Joel is my most favorite person
on the show."

1774
01:35:41,070 --> 01:35:43,938
- The only person.
- Yeah, I'm the only person.

1775
01:35:43,972 --> 01:35:47,175
Anyway,
"I love the adlibs they all say.

1776
01:35:47,208 --> 01:35:51,047
I'd watch MST 3000 any time
'til it goes off."

1777
01:35:51,080 --> 01:35:52,213
That's hopeful.

1778
01:35:52,247 --> 01:35:53,280
"Thank whoever made it up.

1779
01:35:53,313 --> 01:35:55,417
"Please try to get me an address
where I can write.

1780
01:35:55,450 --> 01:35:58,853
Thank you so much.
Sarah McCall."

1781
01:35:58,887 --> 01:36:00,555
So we got an address for you.

1782
01:36:00,588 --> 01:36:01,422
Crow, you want
to give it to them?

1783
01:36:01,455 --> 01:36:03,458
Cambot,
put it up on the screen there.

1784
01:36:03,492 --> 01:36:08,030
- The MST 3000 Fan Club...

1785
01:36:11,867 --> 01:36:14,237
- Well, sirs, that's the end
of our experiment.

1786
01:36:14,270 --> 01:36:15,872
What do you think?

1787
01:36:15,905 --> 01:36:19,007
- Oh, I'm beside myself. Clay?

1788
01:36:19,040 --> 01:36:21,243
- Oh, I'm thrilled to my marrow.

1789
01:36:21,277 --> 01:36:24,045
- I'm happy. Ow!

1790
01:36:24,078 --> 01:36:25,413
- I'll file this.

1791
01:36:25,447 --> 01:36:29,152
- Yes, do that. Well,
until next time, Team Dream.

